iv
Abstract
Urban centers face increasing pressure to achieve carbon neutrality, necessitating 
innovative renewable heating and cooling approaches. Shallow geothermal energy 
systems, specifically closed-loop borehole heat exchangers (BHE), offer substantial 
potential for sustainable energy provision. This master's thesis investigates the impact 
of climate change on the geothermal potential of closed-loop systems by evaluating 
heat extraction and available geothermal power over future time horizons, employing 
climate projections derived from CMIP6 (Coupled Model Intercomparison Project 
Phase 6) via Google Earth Engine (GEE).
In this thesis, a Python-based analytical model was developed to systematically 
simulate heat extraction and geothermal power output of shallow geothermal systems. 
The analysis spans three operational scenarios, 50 years, 100 years and 100 years 
sustainable extraction periods under different climate change trajectories (Shared 
Socioeconomic Pathways SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5). The model incorporated fixed 
key parameters, including borehole length, thermal conductivity, borehole diameter, 
and minimum fluid temperatures, ensuring a controlled assessment focused explicitly 
on climate-induced subsurface temperature variations.
Global-scale ground temperature data were processed from CMIP6 model ensembles 
and utilized as input for the Python simulations, following theoretical frameworks 
outlined in recent studies on groundwater warming and geothermal potential 
estimation methods. Spatial and temporal variations in geothermal yield were 
visualized through interactive maps created using the folium library, providing clear 
representations of scenario-based geothermal energy potential across various 
locations and climate projections. Statistical analyses were conducted to compute 
mean values and the 25th, 50th, and 75th percentiles, thus capturing the uncertainty 
and variability inherent in climate model projections.
Results indicate that future climate warming substantially influences subsurface 
temperatures, enhancing geothermal extraction potential. Specifically, simulations 
revealed average increases of shallow ground temperatures by 1.7 ± 0.6 °C (SSP 2–
4.5) to 3.1 ± 1.1 °C (SSP 5–8.5). Each 1 °C of warming effectively substitutes ~4 m of 
drilling depth for equivalent heat supply, significantly boosting geothermal heat 
extraction efficiency by approximately 8% to 24% by the year 2100 compared to 
current baseline conditions. Areas with higher urbanization rates exhibited greater 
gains due to local thermal anomalies and reduced heating demands. Conversely, 
northern latitudes and permafrost regions demonstrated minimal change due to 
persistent cold conditions limiting subsurface heat recharge.

v
The study highlights the critical role of integrating climate-model projections and 
geospatial analysis with numerical heat-transfer modeling, supporting informed 
decision-making for sustainable urban planning. By quantitatively assessing the longterm geothermal potential under changing climatic conditions, this thesis underscores 
the viability and resilience of shallow geothermal energy as a cornerstone for future 
urban decarbonization strategies.

