Introduction
1
1. Introduction
1.1. Background
In recent decades, global climate policy has centered around mitigating greenhouse 
gas emissions, with the overarching goal of achieving climate neutrality. Urban 
environments lie at the core of this transformation. According to Dodman (2009), cities 
consume nearly three-quarters of the world’s energy and are responsible for the 
majority of global carbon dioxide (CO₂) emissions. This makes them both a challenge 
and an opportunity in the pursuit of decarbonization.
According to NOAA (2025), global average surface temperatures have risen by 
approximately 1.1 °C since the late 19th century, with warming accelerating in recent 
decades. In Germany, the annual mean air temperature has increased even more 
markedly about 1.7 °C from 1881 to 2022 highlighting the strong regional manifestation 
of global warming. The shallow subsurface (down to ~150 m depth) is thermally 
coupled with the atmosphere, meaning that as the climate warms, so do near-surface 
ground temperatures (Wang & Lewis, 1992; Pollack & Chapman, 1993). This warming 
trend is not only evident in the atmosphere but is propagating into the subsurface 
environment. These changes have important implications for shallow geothermal 
systems, which rely on the relatively stable temperatures of the ground for heating and 
cooling. 
According to Bracke et al. (2022), transitioning the heating sector toward sustainability 
is a critical component of climate change mitigation, especially in countries like 
Germany where heating and cooling account for over half of final energy use. As of 
early 2023, more than 80% of Germany’s heat demand is still met by fossil fuels (coal, 
gas, oil), with geothermal and other environmental heat sources contributing only 
around 1.9%. Recognizing this gap, the German government has set ambitious targets 
for its heat energy (Wärmewende) as part of its broader energy transition. By 2030, 
half of all heat supply should be climate-neutral, and by 2045 the heating sector is 
intended to be completely climate-neutral.
Ravidà et al. (2024) explained that shallow geothermal technologies as shown in 
Figure 1.1 are expected to play an important role in achieving these goals. Their 
advantages include a constant and locally available energy source that is independent 
of weather extremes, offering reliable baseload heating/cooling with minimal
greenhouse gas emissions. Indeed, estimates suggest a technically feasible 
geothermal potential on the order of hundreds of terawatt-hours per year in Germany 
with a quarter or more of national heat consumption, if shallow and deep geothermal 

Introduction
2
resources are fully developed. This underscores the significant opportunity for urban 
energy transitions to integrate shallow geothermal systems into future climate-neutral 
cities.
Figure 1.1: Schematic diagram of shallow geothermal energy system (Menberg, K., 
Hemmerle, H., Bayer, P., Bott, P., Bidarmaghz, A., Ferguson, G., Bloemendal, M., Blum, P. 
Opportunities, benefits and impacts of shallow geothermal energy, Nature Reviews Earth and 
Environment, accept-in-principle).
At the same time, climate change itself will alter the boundary conditions under which 
shallow geothermal systems operate. Milder winters and hotter summers could shift 
the balance of heating vs. cooling demand in buildings, while the gradual warming of 
soils and aquifers may change the efficiency and capacity of ground-source heat 
exchange (Walch et al., 2022). 
On one hand, warmer ground temperatures in winter could improve heat pump 
performance by increasing the source temperature, thereby raising COP for heating. 
On the other hand, higher subsurface temperatures year-round mean that injecting 
heat into the ground (for cooling) becomes less efficient, potentially stressing heat 
pump systems as shown in Figure 1.2 in summer (Benz et al., 2024).
This evolving demand profile offers both challenges and synergies for shallow 
geothermal technology. The increased cooling loads present an opportunity to 
recharge the ground with heat in summer (known as seasonal thermal regeneration), 
which can enhance the long-term sustainability and output of geothermal heat pump 
systems (Walch et al., 2022). 
Recent analyses have highlighted that GSHPs for both heating and cooling can 
significantly increase their technical potential, as excess heat from summer cooling is 
stored in the ground and later utilized for winter heating.

Introduction
3
In Germany, for example, the decarbonization of the heating sector is especially 
urgent, currently only about one-fifth of heating energy comes from renewables, 
making heating a major hurdle to reaching the country’s 2045 carbon-neutral target 
(Born et al., 2022). These challenges also present opportunities, as urban centers can 
leverage innovative solutions to reduce emissions on scale.
Figure 1.2: Schematic diagram of a heat pump operating for refrigeration using vapour 
compression cycle (Gil et al., 2022).
Shallow geothermal energy typically refers to ground source heat pump systems and 
underground thermal energy storage in the upper ~400 m of the Earth’s crust operates 
within ground temperatures generally ranging from about 5 °C to 25 °C (Sanner, 2015).
By using electrically driven heat pumps, these systems can transfer heat from the

Introduction
4
ground to buildings in winter and dissipate heat into the ground in summer, providing 
a renewable, low-carbon form of heating and cooling.
One promising solution compatible with dense urban environments is shallow 
geothermal energy, particularly closed-loop ground source heat pump systems 
employing BHEs. Such closed-loop systems transfer heat from the subsurface via 
sealed U-shaped pipes in vertical boreholes, often reaching depths up to 400 meters, 
without extracting groundwater. Such systems are environmentally friendly, require 
minimal surface area, and offer stable thermal performance, which is particularly 
valuable for heating and cooling in densely populated regions (Kammen & Sunter, 
2016).
According to Rivera et al. (2017) among the two broad configurations of geothermal 
systems as shown in Figure 1.3, open-loop and closed-loop, the latter is gaining 
increasing popularity in urban areas due to its controllability and independence from 
aquifer permeability. In closed-loop systems, heat transfer fluids circulate through 
sealed pipes (BHEs) that are embedded into the ground. These fluids exchange heat 
with the surrounding earth, either extracting warmth in winter or dissipating heat in 
summer emphasize the long-term reliability of these systems under varying 
operational and climatic conditions.
Heating and cooling account for a significant portion of energy use in buildings, and 
shallow geothermal heat pumps have been identified as a key technology to reduce 
fossil fuel consumption in this sector. There are already over 400,000 ground-source 
heat pump systems installed in Germany, and recent analyses suggest that 
geothermal heat pumps could technically supply up to 70-75% of Germany’s space 
heating and hot water demand in the future (Born et al., 2022).
In evaluating the feasibility and sustainability of such geothermal systems, the concept 
of geothermal potential becomes pivotal. As Bayer et al. (2019) outline, geothermal 
potential can be assessed in terms of both theoretical and technical energy. 
Theoretical potential reflects the maximum physically available energy, while technical 
potential considers practical constraints such as drilling limits, ground conditions, heat 
pump efficiency, and economic factors. Proper estimation of technical potential thus 
demands detailed modeling that accounts for subsurface properties, system design, 
and environmental variability.
Given this evolving background, incorporating climate change scenarios into 
geothermal assessments is no longer optional; it is essential for accurate long-term 
planning. Without doing so, estimations of geothermal performance risk are outdated

Introduction
5
or overly conservative in a warming world. The interplay between a warming climate 
and shallow geothermal systems is complex, requiring careful study to ensure these 
systems remain efficient and effective under future conditions.
Figure 1.3: (a) Scheme of closed-loop (GSHP system) and (b) open-loop (GWHP system) 
shallow geothermal system (Stauffer et al., 2013).
1.2. Impact of climate change on shallow geothermal systems
Shallow geothermal systems are strongly influenced by the thermal gradient of the 
ground, which in turn is affected by atmospheric temperatures over time. As climate 
change shifts these thermal regimes, understanding and quantifying this influence 
becomes central to predicting long-term system behavior.
Climate change is already measurably affecting the shallow subsurface thermal 
regime in many regions. Numerous studies have documented long-term upward 
trends in ground temperatures and groundwater warmth in response to rising air 
temperatures (Hemmerle & Bayer, 2020).
For instance, in southern Germany (Bavaria), recent repeated borehole 
measurements revealed that shallow groundwater has been warming over the past 
few decades in step with atmospheric warming (Bense et al., 2017). Median 
groundwater temperature at 20 m depth rises by approximately 0.28 K per decade 
since the early 1990s, compared to about 0.35 K per decade increase in ambient air 
temperature over the same period. Even at 60 m depth, a slight but detectable 
warming (~0.09 K per decade) has been observed (Hemmerle & Bayer, 2020).

Introduction
6
Such subsurface warming signals were historically small or negligible in pristine 
aquifers, so their detection now is a clear indication of recent climate change 
penetrating underground. 
Notably, the translation of atmospheric warming to subsurface warming is a gradual 
process subject to thermal diffusion and subsurface heterogeneity. There can be 
damping, and lag effect mean ground surface temperatures do not necessarily rise in 
lockstep with mean air temperatures due to factors like soil moisture, vegetation, or 
snow cover changes that modulate heat exchange at the ground surface (Kurylyk & 
Irvine, 2019). Nevertheless, over multi-year to decadal timescales, a warming climate 
will propagate into the shallow ground, raising the baseline temperatures that closedloop geothermal systems rely on for heat exchange.
Importantly, this stored thermal energy in the shallow ground is not insignificant. 
Hemmerle and Bayer, (2020) estimated that the annual heat gain occurring in 
groundwater below 15 m depth in Bavaria (due to climate-induced warming) is on the 
order of one-third of the region’s yearly heat demand. In other words, a substantial 
amount of thermal energy is being naturally accumulated in the shallow subsurface 
because of global warming, energy that could potentially be harnessed by geothermal 
heat pump systems as a renewable heat source.
This finding underlines a paradoxical aspect of climate change for geothermal energy.
While climate change poses risks, it may also enhance the low carbon geothermal 
potential in some areas by preheating the ground. Indeed, recent global work suggests 
that groundwater warming can have positive effects by effectively charging the shallow 
subsurface with heat that geothermal systems could recycle for heating purposes. 
Benz et al. (2024) argues that the heat accumulating underground from recent climate 
change could sustainably satisfy a share of local heating needs if tapped, although it 
cautions that conversely this warming will reduce efficiency for cooling applications. 
Beyond these energy implications, rising ground temperatures have several other 
consequences that must be considered. The subsurface thermal environment plays a 
crucial role in groundwater quality and ecosystem health (Riedel, 2019). Higher 
groundwater temperatures can accelerate geochemical reactions and microbial 
activity, potentially mobilize contaminants or alter water quality (for example, by 
reducing dissolved oxygen) (Cogswell & Heiss, 2021).
In aquifers that serve as drinking water sources, sustained warming might push 
temperatures beyond regulatory thresholds for potable water, a concern highlighted 
by recent global assessments (Bonte et al., 2013). There are also ecological impacts

Introduction
7
such as many cold adapted subterranean species and groundwater dependent 
ecosystems that could be threatened as their habitat temperatures shift beyond 
physiological tolerance limits (Retter et al., 2021).
GSHPs derive their high efficiency from the relatively stable ground temperature, 
which in temperate regions is cooler than summer air and warmer than winter air
(Stauffer et al., 2013). As climate change nudges ground temperatures upward, winter 
heating mode may initially benefit (Epting et al., 2018) since extracting heat from a 
slightly warmer ground is easier), but the benefit could diminish over time if summers 
are not sufficiently re-cooling the ground. In prolonged heat waves or in scenarios 
without enough seasonal recharge of the ground, the thermal gradient driving heat 
exchange can weaken. Moreover, in cooling dominated scenarios (e.g. in urban areas 
or a future with intense summer heat), the ground may become a heat sink that is 
gradually warming each year (Benz at al., 2022).
Recent research indicates that increased subsurface temperatures will make 
underground cooling less efficient, as heat pumps struggle to reject heat into warmer 
ground (Eyring et al., 2016). If not managed, this could lead to positive feedback where 
warm ground temperatures from climate change and heavy cooling use further impair 
the system’s ability to shed heat, emphasizing the need for proper design (such as 
using larger ground heat exchangers or seasonal borehole thermal storage to balance 
heat flows).
In addition to general climate warming, localized human impacts, especially in urban 
environments compound the situation. Cities often exhibit SUHI, where underground 
temperatures are significantly elevated due to factors like building basements, 
underground infrastructure, surface paving, and waste heat from buildings and 
transportation (Ferguson & Woodbury, 2004). Urban aquifers have been found to 
contain large heat anomalies. For example, studies in German and other Central 
European cities have documented groundwater temperatures several degrees above 
the natural background because of dense urban development (Menberg et al., 2013)
and (Zhu et al., 2015). This anthropogenic subsurface warming, while sometimes 
considered a form of thermal pollution, can also be viewed as a resource. It represents 
low grade heat that can be extracted by geothermal heat pumps (a concept known as 
shallow heat recycling) outlined by Epting et al., (2018).
Menberg et al. (2013) and subsequent researchers showed that these urban thermal 
plumes can increase the geothermal potential of cities, effectively turning city centers 
into giant low temperature heat reservoirs. Climate change will act on top of these 
urban effects even rural groundwater is warming slowly from atmospheric trends 

Introduction
8
alone, but urban ground warming is more pronounced due to the combination of 
climate and local heat inputs. The evolving challenge is to quantify how much overall 
warming (climate driven plus urban) the shallow subsurface will experience, and to 
determine how geothermal system design should adapt. For instance, ground loop 
heat exchangers might need to be installed deeper or spaced farther apart in future 
installations to access cooler ground or to prevent thermal interference, especially 
under high climate warming scenarios.
To anticipate these changes, researchers are increasingly using modeling tools and 
scenario analyses. Sophisticated heat transport models have been developed to 
simulate how subsurface temperatures respond to changes in surface climate over 
decades. A recent global modeling effort by Benz et al. (2024) employed a thermal 
diffusion model coupled with climate projections from the latest CMIP6 to predict 
groundwater temperature changes down to 100 m depth through the end of the 21st 
century (Eyring et al., 2016).
Their simulations, forced under moderate and high emission scenarios (e.g. SSP 2-
4.5 and SSP 5-8.5), indicate that shallow groundwater worldwide could warm by 
roughly 2 to 3.5 °C by 2100, with the magnitude depending on the emissions trajectory. 
While the most extreme warming is projected for high latitude and continental interiors, 
even Central Europe is expected to see substantial subsurface temperature increases 
by the end of 2100. Such modeling underscores that the thermal conditions which 
shallow geothermal systems will encounter in a few decades could be significantly 
different from those of today. Incorporating these projections is thus essential for longterm planning of geothermal installations. 
Additionally, modern data tools like remote sensing and cloud computing are being 
leveraged to assess and visualize these impacts. For example, the results of the Benz 
et al. (2024), study were made accessible through an interactive Google Earth Engine
(GEE) application, which provides zoomable maps of current and future groundwater 
temperatures at various depths under different climate scenarios. This kind of platform 
can aid urban planners and engineers in identifying potential hotspots where ground 
temperatures might exceed desirable ranges for efficient heat pump operation, or 
conversely, areas where there is untapped warming that could be used beneficially.
The influence of these temperature trajectories is then examined analytical model 
grounded in the MFLS method developed by Rivera et al. (2017). This model 
calculates geothermal heat extraction rates based on surface temperature evolution, 
thermal properties of the ground, borehole design parameters, and minimum fluid 
temperature thresholds. The integration of advanced geospatial modeling, climate

Introduction
9
science, and geothermal energy makes this approach uniquely suited for evaluating 
the future viability of shallow geothermal systems. It bridges the gap between abstract 
climate projections and concrete energy planning.
Climate change exerts a multifaceted influence on shallow geothermal systems. It 
alters the subsurface resource (by warming the ground and changing natural heat 
flows) and changes building energy demand patterns, all of which feed back into 
system performance. The net impact is complex: there may be slight efficiency gains 
for heating and increased thermal energy availability in the ground, but also higher 
stresses for cooling and possible regulatory/environmental constraints if groundwater 
becomes too warm. These impacts need to be thoroughly understood and quantified, 
particularly in the context of Germany’s geologic and climatic setting. Ultimately, this 
knowledge will inform, how design, regulate, and strategically deploy shallow 
geothermal installations as part of a climate resilient energy infrastructure.
1.3. Research objectives
This thesis is aiming to assess how ongoing and future climate change will influence 
the technical geothermal potential of shallow closed loop systems in Germany. The 
specific objectives are:
1. Develop a climate-sensitive geothermal modeling framework
A comprehensive python based semi analytical model is constructed using surface 
temperature data from CMIP6 climate projections processed in GEE. The model 
focuses on three operational lifetimes, 50 years, 100 years and 100 years sustainable, 
and accounts for borehole design constraints and thermal boundaries relevant to 
closed loop systems. In this study, sustainability is explicitly defined as the capability 
of the geothermal system to operate over extended periods (e.g., 100 years) without 
causing long-term subsurface temperature decline. This sustainability is achieved 
through balancing annual heat extraction with either natural or artificially enhanced 
thermal recharge, ensuring thermal equilibrium. Such equilibrium prevents 
progressive cooling, thereby enabling continuous, renewable utilization of geothermal 
energy.
2. Visualize geothermal energy potential dynamically and geospatially
Using folium, a suite of interactive maps is generated, showing regional and temporal 
variations in heat extraction and power. These visualizations provide clear insight into 
where and how geothermal systems might be most effective.
3. Quantify uncertainty and variability across climate scenarios

Introduction
10
By aggregating results from multiple climate models under two emissions pathways, 
statistical percentiles (mean, 25th, 50th, 75th) are computed to assess the robustness 
of results. This approach captures the natural variability in climate models and 
provides a range of outcomes for planning purposes.
4. Determine how future warming affects technical geothermal potential
By comparing future ground temperatures to baseline conditions, the research 
identifies areas where geothermal system performance could improve. This also helps 
inform strategic deployment of such systems in urban centers across Germany, where 
heating and cooling demands are both concentrated and rising.

Material and methods
11
2. Material and methods
2.1. Study area
The scope of this study encompasses the entirety of Germany, centrally located in 
Western Europe between approximately 47° and 55°N latitude and 6° to 15°E 
longitude. Covering an area of about 357,000 square kilometers, Germany exhibits a 
wide range of geological and climatic conditions, making it an ideal setting to 
investigate the spatial variability of shallow geothermal energy potential using closed
loop BHE systems.
Geologically, Germany sits atop a mosaic of tectonic units that shape its subsurface 
heat flow and hydrogeological properties. The northern third of the country is 
dominated by the North German Basin, characterized by thick sequences of 
unconsolidated Quaternary and Tertiary sediments as shown in Figure 2.1. These 
deposits offer high porosity and moderate thermal conductivities, making them 
favorable for geothermal systems (Agemar et al., 2014; Stober & Bucher, 2013). 
Toward the southwest, the Upper Rhine Graben, a Cenozoic rift structure, is known 
for its elevated geothermal gradient and fractured rock aquifers, both of which 
contribute to enhanced thermal output and potential for both shallow and deep 
geothermal use (Van et al., 2021).
In southern and southeastern regions, crystalline basement formations such as those 
in the Black Forest and the Bavarian Forest present a different hydrogeological 
environment. These metamorphic and igneous rocks typically have lower porosity but 
relatively high thermal conductivity, resulting in slower but more consistent heat 
exchange profiles for BHE systems (Paschen et al., 2003). Transition zones and 
sedimentary basins surrounding these highlands further increase geological 
heterogeneity and require localized calibration of geothermal models.
In terms of climate, Germany spans several transitional climatic zones from oceanic 
influences in the northwest, characterized by mild winters and moderate summer 
temperatures, to more continental patterns in the east and southeast with colder 
winters and warmer summers as shown in Figure 2.2. 
This climatic variability significantly impacts both surface and shallow subsurface 
temperatures, which are the boundary conditions critical to the performance of GSHP
systems. Long term changes in these surface conditions under climate change are 
projected to alter the thermal recharge of the shallow subsurface (Rivera et al., 2017;
Benz et al., 2024), thereby influencing the long-term sustainability and yield of

Material and methods
12
geothermal systems. To account for these multifactorial controls, the present study 
adopts a high-resolution (5 km per pixel) spatial modeling framework using 
downscaled CMIP6 climate model outputs, processed and applied across Germany’s 
geodiversity landscape. By combining geological, different parameters, and climate 
datasets, the aim is to deliver an analytically grounded and spatially resolved 
understanding of the sustainable heat extraction potential of closed loop geothermal 
systems under evolving environmental conditions.
