Material and methods
13
2.2. Methodological workflow
The methodological structure of this study combines climate data processing, 
geothermal heat modeling, and geospatial analysis into a coherent, replicable 
framework. Each phase is designed to progressively build upon the previous, ensuring 
that the modeling outputs are grounded in climate science while remaining technically 
relevant for energy planning and sustainable resource assessment. Figure 2.3
provides a visual overview of the complete workflow.
The process began with the acquisition of future climate projection data, sourced from 
the CMIP6. Specifically, this study used data from 8 GCMs available on the GEE 
platform, selected under two representative concentration pathway scenarios. SSP 2-
4.5, and SSP 5-8.5. Once retrieved, the raw climate data underwent a careful 
preprocessing phase. First, all raster layers were converted to a consistent spatial 
reference system, namely EPSG:4326 (WGS 84), to ensure compatibility with 
geospatial tools and subsequent python-based analysis. 
The next phase involved the implementation of a numerical model to evaluate 
geothermal heat extraction potential. This was achieved using a custom python 
notebook built upon analytical solutions to heat transfer problems in BHE systems. 
The model follows the FLS theory, as proposed by Rivera et al. (2017), and 
incorporates contemporary climate-driven subsurface temperature trends discussed 
in Benz et al. (2024). By integrating fixed subsurface parameters and fluid temperature 
thresholds, the model isolates the impact of climate change from design variability, 
offering a controlled basis for scenario analysis.
Three primary simulation scenarios were defined to reflect different temporal horizons 
of system performance. The first, a 50 year “finite-life” scenario, evaluates geothermal 
systems operating with a defined thermal debt, the second, a 100 year and the third, 
a 100 years sustainable scenario.To contextualize and interpret the simulation 
outputs, a geospatial visualization layer was integrated using the folium library based 
on JavaScript. It enables users to navigate across regions of Germany and visually 
assess changes in energy yield under different timeframes and climate futures.
Finally, the results from the model were subjected to a statistical evaluation. For each 
grid cell and scenario, key summary statistics, including the mean, 25th percentile, 50th
percentile, and 75th percentile, were computed across the climate model ensemble.
The process is explained in detail in the following sections of this chapter. 

Material and methods
14
Figure 2.3: Flowchart of semi-analytical model and simulation workflow.

Material and methods
15
2.3. Climate data and GEE
To incorporate climate change effects into the geothermal model, this work integrates 
data from the latest generation of climate projections and utilizes modern 
computational tools for analysis and visualization. Specifically, CMIP6 climate model 
outputs, Python simulation, and interactive geospatial mapping platforms as described 
below.
Future ground surface temperature trajectories are derived from the CMIP6 scenarios. 
The published projections are used under two representative pathways, a “modest 
mitigation” scenario (SSP 2-4.5) and a “high emissions” scenario (SSP 5-8.5), 
consistent with the latest IPCC assessments. These scenarios correspond to 
approximately +2.5 °C and +4.5 °C global warming by 2100, respectively (relative to 
preindustrial), though regional outcomes vary. 
For Germany, CMIP6 models project substantial warming, especially in summer, and 
a rise in mean annual temperature on the order of 2-5 °C by late century depending 
on the scenario (Eyring et al., 2016). Rather than directly simulating complex landclimate processes, these scenarios applied by adjusting the ground surface 
temperature input in this model.
The approach aligns with recent global studies that use standard climate model 
outputs to drive subsurface heat simulations. For instance, Benz et al. (2024) forced 
a global heat diffusion model with CMIP6 data and projected that shallow groundwater 
temperatures. By incorporating analogous data, the model captures the climateadaptive aspect, as the climate warms, ground temperatures rise, and the BHE heat 
extraction potential is dynamically affected.
GEE is a cloud-based platform for planetary scale geospatial analysis that hosts a vast 
catalog of satellite imagery and climate data (Gorelick et al., 2017). By using GEE’s 
JavaScript API, extracted information like land surface temperature trends, ground 
surface temperature, and downscaled climate model outputs for this study region. This 
approach leverages GEE’s ability to handle large datasets and perform on the fly 
computations in the cloud.
This approach leverages GEE’s ability to handle large datasets and perform on the fly 
computations in the cloud. Furthermore, the use of GEE in academic studies is on the 
rise, Benz et al. (2024) even developed an interactive GEE web application to visualize 
subsurface warming projections globally. This underscores the credibility of this
approach to using GEE for data handling and dissemination.

Material and methods
16
This study incorporates advanced climate data projections from the CMIP6, which 
serves as the current benchmark for global climate modeling in academic and policy 
contexts. The projections were accessed via GEE, enabling automated, highresolution spatiotemporal data extraction and harmonization. Two key climate 
pathways were selected, the intermediate emission trajectory (ssp245 band) and the 
high-emission scenario (ssp585 band), extending from the period (2000) to (2100). 
These pathways represent different socio-economic futures and GHGs emission 
levels.
To ensure consistency in spatial analysis, climate raster outputs from 8 different 
CMIP6 GCMs were resampled and projected to EPSG:4326 (WGS 84). The climate 
raster data derived from eight different CMIP6 models, namely BBC, CanESM, GFDL, 
GISS, HadGEM, IPSL, MIROC, and MPI, covering a temporal range from January 1, 
2000, to December 31, 2100. Although the original datasets from these CMIP6 models 
varied in spatial resolution and included multiple climate bands, they have been 
uniformly preprocessed in this study to a standardized spatial resolution of 5 km². 
Additionally, two specific climate scenarios or bands ssp585 and ssp245 are extracted 
and provided in GeoTIFF format. This preprocessing ensures consistency and 
comparability across all models when utilized in subsequent geospatial analyses.
This resolution strikes a balance between computational efficiency and spatial fidelity, 
enabling localized insights while preserving regional coverage. The inclusion of distinct 
GCMs allowed the modeling framework to capture inter model variability, thus 
supporting uncertainty quantification.
2.4. Geological, hydrogeological, and thermal parameters
The performance of BHEs is governed by several thermal, hydraulic, and geological 
variables. In this study, parameter values were selected to reflect real-world system 
designs and were primarily drawn from Bayer et al. (2016), Stauffer et al. (2013) and 
Benz et al. (2024), both of which conducted field-validated geothermal assessments 
in central Europe.
All parameter values for the model have been carefully selected based on authoritative 
literature and site-specific data. Table 2.1 summarizes the key parameters for the 
reference scenario, which reflects typical shallow subsurface conditions in Germany 
(and more broadly central Europe) under urban or non-urban settings. These values 
are derived from measurements and recommendations in prior studies and ensure 
that the model outputs are grounded in reality:

Material and methods
17
Thermal conductivity (λ): This represents a mix of saturated unconsolidated 
sediments (sand/gravel with groundwater) common in German subsurface geology. A 
moderate to high value means the ground can conduct heat relatively well, which 
generally improves BHE performance by transporting heat from the far field more 
effectively. Higher conductivity reduces the temperature drop around the borehole for 
a given heat extraction rate, thereby sustaining higher extraction rates. The chosen 
value (2.5 W/m·K) is supported by site-specific studies in Zurich (Bayer et al., 2016) 
and falls within typical ranges in VDI 4640 guidelines for moist sediments (VDI, 2012).
Thermal diffusivity (am): This value corresponds to the above conductivity combined 
with a volumetric heat capacity which is typical for water-saturated soil. Diffusivity 
governs the rate at which thermal disturbances spread in the ground. The selected
value 2.5 W/m·K implies that it takes on the order of months for a seasonal heat pulse 
to penetrate ~10 m depth (Kurylyk & Irvine, 2019), and decades to reach ~100 m, 
consistent with observations (Bense & Kurylyk, 2017). A smaller value would mean 
slower diffusion (greater thermal storage, sharper gradients around the BHE), whereas 
a larger value would disperse heat faster but reduce the ground’s ability to store heat. 
The chosen value is representative of central Europe (Bayer et al., 2016) and ensures 
the model’s temporal responses (e.g. the characteristic time) match real thermal 
regimes.
Geothermal gradient (k): This is a moderate geothermal gradient indicating that in 
undisturbed conditions, temperature increases by ~3 °C per 150 m depth. This value 
matches measured temperature depth profiles in the region (Bayer et al., 2016). 
Although the gradient contributes only a small portion to shallow ground temperatures, 
it becomes more relevant for deeper boreholes. A higher value would mean warmer 
deep ground (beneficial for extraction), but in the shallow context (50-200 m) the 
difference is modest and includes (k) setting correct initial conditions and accounting
for the slight increase of undisturbed temperature with depth, which in turn enhances 
extraction at greater depths (warmer rock yields more heat per meter drilled).
Borehole depth (H): It is a crucial design parameter, directly proportional to the heat 
exchanger capacity. The considered 150 m (near the upper practical limit for vertical 
BHEs in this context) outlined by Taniguchi et al. (2007). The upper limit of 150 m is 
chosen based on two considerations: (1) observed urban subsurface warming typically 
extends to 100 - 150 m (Eggleston, 2025) so 200 m ensures capturing the full vertical 
extent of anthropogenic warming; (2) beyond ~200 m, the assumption of a uniform 
heat extraction along the borehole becomes less accurate, as higher rock 
temperatures at depth would yield higher local extraction if unconstrained (Holmberg

Material and methods
18
et al., 2015). By capping (H) at 150 m minimizing model error from this effect, 
consistent with recommendations for deeper boreholes may require segmented 
analysis.
Borehole radius (rb): the radius (rb = 0.1 m) reflects standard borehole construction
outlined by Stauffer et al. (2013) and Bayer et al. (2016). A consistent radius is used 
across scenarios so that differences in performance are not due to borehole size but 
rather operational or environmental factors. In general, a larger radius (wider borehole) 
would slightly reduce borehole thermal resistance but would also reduce the initial 
temperature response for a given (q) (spreading heat extraction over a larger 
circumference).
Borehole thermal resistance (Rtb): This effective thermal resistance accounts for 
the thermal coupling between the borehole fluid and the surrounding ground. The
chosen value (0.15 m.K/W) corresponds to a typical U-tube BHE with standard grout 
and flow rate. It is in line with reported values for long-term operation outlined by Bayer 
et al. (2016) and Claesson & Eskilson, (1988), found 0.1-0.2 m·K/W for 150 m 
boreholes. A lower value would indicate a more efficient borehole (less temperature 
drops from fluid to ground), which would improve heat extraction for the same. 
Conversely, a higher value (e.g. due to poor grouting or tubing) would decrease
performance. By selecting 0.15, assumed a moderately well-designed BHE.
Fixed Initial ground temperature (Ts): This is the approximate annual mean ground 
surface temperature for the reference location in recent decades. It sets the baseline 
from which any ground surface warming is measured. For instance, an increase of 
(Tm) means annual ground temperature of 3 °C would imply the new average GST is 
13 °C. The 10 °C is used so that the initial ground temperature at shallow depths aligns 
with observations (e.g., ~10 °C at 0 m, ~11.5 °C at 50 m, etc). If Ts were significantly 
higher or lower, it would alter and could shift the absolute fluid temperatures, but the 
relative performance changes due to warming would remain similar. The chosen 10 
°C is site-specific (e.g., Zurich averages ~10 °C and is representative of the midlatitude climate in the study area studied by Bayer et al. (2016).

