Material and methods
20
With the above parameters defined the two primary established operational scenarios 
to evaluate the BHE performance, following the methodology of Rivera et al. (2017):
2.5. Operational scenarios
Depleting Scenario: A finite operational period of 50 years at a constant heat extraction 
rate, at the end of which the system is considered exhausted (i.e. underground heat 
depletion reaches a design limit). This scenario mimics a conservative planning 
approach where the BHE is sized to provide a certain heat supply for a project lifetime 
of 5 decades. The impose of SIA 384/6 standard criterion that the mean fluid 
temperature must not drop below -1.5 °C after 50 years of operation This safety 
threshold (from the Swiss norm SIA 384/6 (SIA, 2010) is intended to prevent ground 
freezing and ensure heat pump efficiency. Using this model in practice, found (q) by 
iteration or root-finding such that Tmin at 50 years is exactly -1.5 °C, given the chosen
rb and the warming scenario (Tm). This scenario represents maximizing energy 
extraction in a non-recharged system over a fixed time and thus corresponds to a nonrenewable use of the geothermal resource.
Renewable Scenario: A long term 100 years operational period (steady-state cycling) 
where the annual heat extraction is balanced by natural or artificial heat inputs, so that 
the subsurface temperature does not drift over the long term (Ungemach et al., 1988) 
and (Hähnlein et al., 2013). In this scenario, the extraction rate (q) is lower than in the 
depleting case, chosen such that after a sufficiently long time, the ground reaches a 
new equilibrium without progressive cooling. Mathematically, q can be defined as the 
rate for which borehole wall temperature approaches a stable value as t under 
constant Tm forcing. Equivalently, q is the renewable heat yield that can be supported 
indefinitely by the combination of natural heat flow (geothermal gradient) and surface 
heat influx. Rivera et al. (2017) characterize renewability as the condition where the 
ratio of renewable to depleting extraction is such that ground heat depletion is fully 
compensated. In this implementation, determined q by requiring that Tmin does not 
continue to decrease after many decades (or that it meets the -1.5 °C limit). This often 
involves finding the q for which the 50 years temperature drop is mostly recovered by 
the surface heat recharge. Renewable scenario results give insight into the 
sustainable heat supply capacity if seasonal or long-term recharging (e.g., by climate 
warming or summer heat injection) balances the extraction.
Each scenario is evaluated for the range of borehole lengths and surface warming 
values defined above and computed the technical geothermal potential in each case, 
which is defined as the mean heat extraction rate per borehole (or per meter of 
borehole) that can be achieved under the given constraints. By comparing scenario 

Material and methods
21
outcomes, quantified net gains are attributable to ground warming. For example, in the 
depleting scenario, an increase in Tm tends to allow a higher q before hitting the -
1.5 °C fluid limit, thereby increasing the 50 years heat extraction total. In the renewable 
scenario, a higher Tm translates to a greater steady heat flux from the surface, which 
can support a larger continuous q without net cooling (Menberg et al., 2013) and (Benz
et al., 2015). These effects are analyzed in Chapter 3.
2.6. Python based semi-analytical framework
All modeling steps and analyses have been implemented using the python 
programming language and its scientific libraries. Python was chosen for its versatility, 
reproducibility, and the strong ecosystem of libraries for scientific computing (e.g. 
numpy, scipy, joblib), data handling (geopandas, xarray), and visualization (matplotlib, 
rasterio, folium). A structured Python workflow allows a seamless integration of
geospatial data processing, analytical solutions, and scenario analysis. This approach 
is increasingly common in geothermal research; for example, Walch et al. (2022) 
developed a regional GSHP potential model entirely in python, combining geospatial 
mapping, analytical heat transfer calculations, and demand modeling.
Following similar principles, a pipeline was constructed where (1) climate and ground 
temperature input data are implemented, (2) the semi analytical model 
(Equations 2.5.1 - 2.5.5) is solved over time for each scenario, and (3) outputs are 
post-processed into folium maps and graphs. The use of python ensures that the 
methodology is transparent and adaptable such as parameters can be changed and 
the model re-run efficiently, facilitating sensitivity analyses. Additionally, python’s 
capability to interface with GIS data is crucial for this study, which involves spatially 
distributed results across Germany. Open-source libraries (including a tool for 
computing g-functions) and custom scripts were utilized to evaluate the FLS integrals. 
These evaluations were validated against known solutions as detailed by Rivera et al. 
(2017) and Eskilson (1987) and apply them over grids of single borehole. All code was 
written in Jupyter notebooks for clarity and will be made available for reproducibility.
In this study, a physic based analytical model is developed to simulate the long-term 
performance of BHEs under varying ground temperature conditions. The subsurface 
domain is idealized as a semi-infinite homogeneous medium with thermal conductivity 
(λ) and thermal diffusivity (am), which is a common assumption for shallow geothermal 
analyses (Rivera et al., 2016). The BHE is represented by a vertical heat source, while 
any GST increase (due to urbanization or climate change) is represented by an 
equivalent surface heat flux (Rivera et al., 2015). Superposition of the thermal effects 
from the BHE and the ground surface allows calculation of temperature at any point 

Material and methods
22
and time in the subsurface. Of particular interest is the temperature at the borehole 
wall because it directly influences the extractable heat rate (q) from the BHE. In 
essence, borehole wall temperature is determined by superposing three components.
(1) the temperature changes from BHE operation, obtained by solving a homogeneous 
heat conduction problem; (2) the temperature changes due to time dependent surface 
boundary conditions, from solving a non-homogeneous boundary problem); and (3) 
the initial undisturbed subsurface temperature as a baseline. The linearity of 
conductive heat transport justifies this superposition principle for combining different 
heat sources.
For the homogeneous problem (no surface warming), the g-function solution relates 
the mean borehole wall temperature to the heat extraction rate. Originally, Eskilson 
(1987) introduced the g-function via numerical simulations for specified borehole 
configurations. The g-function G(tc) is a dimensionless temperature response that 
depends on normalized time and geometry. It allows one to express the borehole wall 
temperature caused by BHE operation.
2.6.1 Mean MFLS - heat extraction rate from a finite line source
Here (q) is the heat extraction rate per unit borehole length (H), (λ) is the soil thermal 
conductivity, (rb) is the borehole radius, (H) is the borehole length, and (t) is a 
characteristic time for heat diffusion along the borehole depth. Time (tc) is measured 
from the start of BHE operation. Physically, time represents the time scale at which 
the thermal front reaches the bottom of a borehole of length. 
The appearance of borehole radius reflects that the borehole’s finite radius provides a 
short-term thermal buffer. However, for long-term performance (beyond the first few 
hours or days), the line-source assumption (infinitely small radius) is valid, and gfunction mainly depends on (tc). A larger borehole radius tends to slightly reduce the 
initial temperature drop by providing more surface area for heat exchange, but its 
impact diminishes over longer time scales as heat penetrates deeper into the ground.
In this model, a radius of 0.1 m is used (which corresponds to a typical borehole 
diameter of 0.2 m) a value representative of standard double U pipe BHE installations. 
This choice is justified by common practice guidelines (Reuss et al., 2006) and 
ensures that short-term thermal transients are reasonably captured while maintaining 
analytical simplicity.
Eskilson’s (1987) original g-function was obtained numerically for specific bore field 
geometries. In contrast, employed an analytical FLS formulation for a single borehole, 

Material and methods
23
which yields a semi-analytical expression. The FLS solution, as derived by Claesson 
& Javed, (2011) and Bandos et al. (2009), integrates the fundamental solution of heat 
conduction over the finite length of the borehole. The result is an integral expression 
for the borehole wall temperature rise due to the BHE, which replaces the numerically 
tabulated g-function with an exact series or integral form. This approach has been 
shown to accurately reproduce g-function results for single boreholes across time 
scales from minutes to decades. An added advantage of the FLS model is that it 
supports superposition with other analytic solutions, enabling them to incorporate time
dependent surface temperature changes in a tractable way. In long-term simulations 
(on the order of years to decades), high-frequency fluctuations in (q) (e.g. hourly or 
daily changes) are not critical thus, the smooth FLS based g-function is appropriate 
for capturing the dominant thermal dynamics (Rivera et al., 2017). Moreover, the 
analytical nature of the solution allows efficient implementation in a GIS or Python 
environment for large-scale mapping.
This function evaluates the mean heat extraction per meter of borehole using the 
MFLS method that incorporates vertical advection and finite borehole depth by Rivera 
et al. (2017):
