2.6.2 GST change in infinite half-space (seasonal effects)
When the ground surface is not held at a constant reference temperature (i.e., when 
surface warming occurs), the non-homogeneous boundary condition at the top of the
domain must be considered. In this model, an additional term (Ta) is superimposed to 

Material and methods
24
account for the Transient Boundary Condition of increased GST. 
Rivera et al. (2015, 2016) derived closed-form solutions for the ground temperature 
response to surface heat flux anomalies using continuous heat sources distributed 
along the surface. The approach was adopted, representing an urban or climateinduced GST increase as a uniform heat input over a specified area of the surface.
The analytical solution as a function of time and depth involves an integral of the error 
function (erf) and exponential terms, characterizing the diffusion of a surface heat 
pulse into the ground.
Rather than reproduce the full derivation here, noted that this solution yields the 
excess temperature at depth due to a surface temperature step-change. For an 
idealized scenario where an entire region experiences a uniform increase in surface 
temperature, the induced vertical heat flux raises subsurface temperatures over time. 
Depth-averaging this effect over the borehole length (H) gives the contribution to the 
mean borehole wall temperature. This simplification yields conservative (first order) 
estimates of the impact of surface warming, which can later be refined with transient 
climate inputs.
Adapted from heat conduction models for semi-infinite media, this equation accounts 
for time-varying background temperatures (e.g., from CMIP6 data) by Rivera et al. 
(2017):
