2.6.3 Initial ground temperature profile (geothermal gradient)
Finally, the initial condition represents the undisturbed ground temperature prior to any 
BHE operation or surface warming. In shallow geothermal contexts, the initial 
temperature profile is often approximated by a steady geothermal gradient 

Material and methods
25
superimposed on the long-term average surface temperature. Thus, the mean ground 
temperature over the borehole length can be estimated as (Tm) (Claesson & Eskilson, 
1988). 
A linear geothermal gradient (k) was assumed, which is typical for the study region’s 
geology, and a background fixed initial ground temperature (Ts) around 10 °C, 
consistent with long-term climate normal in Germany (Bayer et al., 2016). These 
values yield an initial temperature around 10 °C at the surface, increasing to about 
11.5 °C at (H=100 m) (before any anthropogenic warming). The chosen gradient and 
(Ts) align with measurements in unconsolidated sediments beneath central European 
cities and ensure that this model’s baseline matches local ground conditions. 
By superposition of all components, the mean borehole wall temperature in the 
presence of surface heating this model estimates the initial ground temperature as a 
function of depth and geothermal gradient by Rivera et al. (2017).
In contrast, this model allows to evolve under time varying GST inputs, which is crucial 
for climate-adaptive geothermal modeling. Effectively, as the ground surface warms 
over decades, this additional heat influx can offset some of the cooling around the 
borehole caused by heat extraction. The degree of this compensation will depend on 
the magnitude and timing relative to BHE operation, as explored in the following 
sections.
