2.6.4 Mean fluid temperature in borehole heat exchanger
To connect the borehole wall temperature to the heat pump fluid temperature and 
ultimately to practical design limits, introduced thermal resistance. The borehole 
thermal resistance (Rtb) accounts for heat transfer between the borehole wall and the 
circulating fluid (through pipe conduction and convection). Using a quasi-steady 
approximation, the mean fluid temperature (Tm) can be related to borehole wall 
temperature (Hähnlein et al., 2010). 

Material and methods
26
In reality, (Rtb) depends on BHE construction (pipe configuration, grout properties, 
fluid flow rate) and can vary with temperature and time, but for long-term analysis, an 
effective constant value is assumed. Here (Rtb = 0.15), which falls in the expected 
range for typical double U BHEs and was used in prior studies for multi-year operation
(Bayer et al., 2016).
The transient total thermal resistance is sometimes defined to include both borehole 
resistance and the ground’s resistance over time. However, the analysis will primarily 
use (Rtb) and the temperature formulation above, which is sufficient to evaluate 
performance against design criteria (Li & Lai, 2015).
The mean fluid temperature (Tf) in a BHE is computed by combining the three models 
above and subtracting the thermal resistance losses by Rivera et al. (2017):
