2.6.5 Sustainable heat extraction rate calculation
An iterative root-finding algorithm (Brent’s method) is used to determine the maximum 
sustainable heat extraction rate (ql) over long-term operation without violating the 
minimum allowable fluid temperature Tmin by Rivera et al. (2017). The objective 
function is:
𝛥𝑇 = 𝑇𝑓𝑙𝑢𝑖𝑑(𝑡) − 𝑇𝑚𝑖𝑛
2.6.6 Power output calculation
Once the heat extraction rate per unit length ql is computed W/m, the total thermal 
power for a single borehole is computed by:
𝑃 = 𝑞𝑙 ⋅ 𝐻
Where:
H : borehole depth [m]
P : total power per borehole [W]

Material and methods
27
ql : heat extraction rate [W/m]
2.7. Geospatial statistical evaluation and visualization with folium
To communicate the model outcomes, employed interactive geospatial visualization 
using the folium library in python. It builds on the leaflet.js platform to create dynamic 
maps that can be embedded with results data. In this thesis, folium maps are used to 
display the spatial distribution of key results, such as the heat extraction rates and 
power across different regions and the difference in climates scenarios. The 
advantage of interactive maps is that stakeholders and readers can zoom, pan, and 
query specific locations, making the complex results more accessible. This is 
particularly valuable for conveying climate-based projections which may vary 
geographically. As noted by Suma et al. (2024), interactive maps are a powerful 
communication tool, capable of conveying complex spatial patterns to broad 
audiences in an intuitive way. Integration of folium into the Python workflow enabled 
the creation of an interactive, user-friendly map for numerical model output. All maps 
produced are exported as png files, digitized in Inkscape and most of them are 
explained Chapter 3 and rest are included in the Appendix of this thesis.
This approach allows dynamic exploration of results across Germany by layering heat 
extraction rates and power outputs computed from model. Interactive web maps were 
created for each climate scenario SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5. For each scenario, the 
model computed, and outputs were visualized as a distinct map layer:
• Heat extraction rate (50 years)
• Power output (50 years)
• Heat extraction rate (100 years)
• Power output (100 years)
• Sustainable heat extraction rate (100 years)
• Renewable power (100 years)
Each layer was rendered for both SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5 climate scenarios and 
displayed with color-consistent styling:
• Viridis colormap for extraction rates [W/m]
• Red colormap for power outputs [W]
Interactive map layers are bounded by Germany’s EPSG:4326 shapefile and include 
toggleable overlays. Colorbars and tooltips were incorporated to enhance 

Material and methods
28
interpretability, allowing users to examine geographic differences in geothermal 
potential. To ensure robust decision-making, the simulation includes statistical 
analyses of geothermal potential across all scenarios. For each CMIP6 model and 
SSP scenario combination, the following statistical metrics were computed at every 
grid cell:
• Mean value: representing central tendency
• 25th percentile: representing conservative potential
• 50th percentile (median): representing typical expectation
• 75th percentile: representing optimistic scenario
Together, this geospatial statistical workflow enhances the transparency, usability, 
and planning value of climate-adaptive geothermal energy modeling. The interactive 
map illustrates the spatial distribution of:
• Maximum extraction rate [W/m] (50 years)
• Usable power output [W] (50 years)
• Maximum extraction rate [W/m] (100 years)
• Usable power output [W] (100 years)
• Sustainable heat extraction rate [W/m] (100 years)
• Renewable power output [W] (100 years)
The methodology of this chapter represents a robust semi analytical geothermal model 
with data and computational tools. Each parameter in the model has been justified by 
scientific literature and local data, ensuring credibility. Leveraging GEE and folium for 
data acquisition and visualization enables to handle big data and present results in an 
insightful manner to both experts and non-specialists. The inclusion of CMIP6 based 
climate scenarios and SUHIs effects makes the model climate-adaptive, an 
increasingly important feature in sustainable energy planning. The use of python for 
modeling ensures that the analysis is transparent and reproducible, echoing recent 
studies in the field. The following chapter 3 will apply this methodology to quantify the 
climate adaptive geothermal potential in the study area, with comprehensive results 
and discussion of the impacts of various parameters and scenarios.

Results and discussion
29
3. Results and discussion
This section presents the projected geothermal heat extraction rates (in W/m of 
borehole) and the corresponding total usable power (in W per borehole) from 2000 to 
2100 across Germany. Temporal trends in heat extraction rates, differences between 
climate scenarios, spatial variability across Germany, and the statistical distribution 
(25th, 50th, 75th percentiles and means) of the projected geothermal potential were 
highlighted. The statistical significance of the trends was evaluated, the sensitivity of 
outcomes to key parameters was assessed, and the findings were compared with 
various existing literature. The implications for geothermal energy utilization in the 
context of climate change and the energy transition are then discussed.
3.1 Temporal evolution of heat extraction rates under climate change
3.1.1 Mean and 50th percentile (median) of heat extraction rate
Mean maximum heat extraction rates W/m for a single BHE in Figure 3.1 under two 
climate scenarios (SSP 2-4.5 vs. SSP 5-8.5) show the values for 50 years, 100 years, 
and a 100 years sustainable operation (with seasonal thermal recharge), illustrating 
the effect of time scenarios and renewable operation on achievable heat extraction. 
The temporal evolution of BHE heat extraction rates shows that longer operational 
horizons and surface warming trends lead to higher sustainable extraction capacities. 
Figure 3.1: Graphical overview of mean projection for temporal heat extraction rates [W/m] for 
the SSP 2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).

Results and discussion
30
Over 50 years of design life, the mean maximum extraction rate is about 26.97 W/m 
under the mid-range climate scenario (SSP 2-4.5), whereas under the high-emission 
scenario (SSP 5-8.5) it rises slightly to 27.39 W/m (a ~1.6% increase). By the end of 
the 2100 (100 years horizon), warming contributes to a higher extraction rate of 
roughly 31.24 W/m in SSP 5-8.5 versus 31.05 W/m in SSP 2-4.5 (only a ~0.6% 
increase). Notably, when operated in a sustainable manner for 100 years with annual 
thermal recharge, the available extraction per meter rises to around 47.39 W/m in the 
high scenario against 46.05 W/m in the mid scenario, an improvement of ~3% due to 
climate warming.
These results indicate that climate change by 2100 would modestly increase the heat 
yield of shallow geothermal systems (on the order of a few percent), consistent with 
the projected ~+3.5 °C rise in shallow groundwater temperatures under SSP 5-8.5 vs 
~+2.1 °C under SSP 2-4.5.
Figure 3.2 shows the median (50th percentile) of maximum heat extraction rates across 
Germany. This illustrates the central tendency of spatial variation in extraction 
potential under each scenario and time operations.
Figure 3.2: Graphical overview of 50th percentile projection for temporal heat extraction rates 
[W/m] for the SSP 2-4. 5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).
The median extraction rates follow trends like the mean. After 50 years of operation, 
the median maximum extraction is 27.26 W/m (SSP 2-4.5) and 27.68 W/m (SSP 5-
8.5). After 100 years this increases to about 31.4 W/m in the high scenario SSP 5-8.5
vs 31.2 W/m in the lower scenario SSP 2-4.5. Under sustainable operation for 100 

Results and discussion
31
years, the median potential reaches 48.33 W/m (SSP 5-8.5) compared to 46.98 W/m 
(SSP 2-4.5). The climate-driven increment in median values (~1-2 W/m) is comparable 
to that of the mean, reaffirming that a warmer ground by 2100 yields on the order of 2
- 3% higher extraction rates per meter of borehole. In essence, a warmer climate 
moderates the long-term cooling of the ground caused by BHE heat withdrawal, 
allowing slightly greater heat extraction before reaching design temperature limits. 
This effect is evident in the 100 years horizon results, where the ground warming over 
the century compensates for some of the thermal drawdown, resulting in a higher 
permissible rate than in the 50 years case. The implication is that the temporal 
evolution of extraction rates is governed by two competing factors. The progressive 
cooling of the subsurface around the BHE versus the gradual heating of the 
subsurface by climate change. This simulation suggests that by mid to late century, 
the ground warming effect partially offsets thermal depletion, enabling a longer-term 
operation with only minimal loss or even a slight gain in heat extraction performance
(Wang et al., 2009).
3.1.2 25th and 75th percentile of heat extraction rate
The 25th percentile (lower quartile) of maximum heat extraction rates across Germany 
is projected in Figure 3.3. This represents areas with less favorable geothermal 
conditions, highlighting how climate impacts might differ in lower-yield locations. It 
shows generally slightly lower absolute extraction rates, as expected for less favorable 
locations, but a very similar pattern of increase under warming. 
A low yield location (25th percentile) can sustain about 26.63 W/m after 50 years in the 
SSP 2-4.5 scenario, which increases to 26.91 W/m under SSP 5-8.5 (~1% higher). 
Over 100 years, the extraction rises from 30.9 W/m in SSP 2-4.5 to 31.04 W/m in the 
SSP 5-8.5 (~0.5% increase). Under sustainable 100 years operation, the 25th
percentile potential is 46.17 W/m (SSP 5-8.5) vs 44.96 W/m (SSP 2-4.5), an 
improvement of ~2.7%. 
The uniformly positive influence of climate change across percentile levels 
underscores that higher ground temperatures provide a modest boost to BHE output. 
Warmer subsurface conditions enhance heat transfer rates and delay ground freezing, 
thus improving sustainable heat extraction even in areas with initially lower thermal 
potential. However, the small magnitude of these increases (only on the order of 1-
3%) suggests that climate change alone is not a game-changer for individual BHE 
performance, it is a second-order effect compared to site-specific geologic factors. 

Results and discussion
32
Figure 3.3: Graphical overview of 25th percentile projection for temporal heat extraction rates 
[W/m] for the SSP 2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).
Figure 3.4 for the 75th percentile (upper quartile) of maximum heat extraction rates 
reflects higher yielding values and how much they can benefit from warming and longterm operation. Higher-yield (Figure 3.4) naturally support greater extraction rates, 
with or without climate change.
Figure 3.4: Graphical overview of 75th percentile projection for temporal heat extraction rates 
[W/m] for the SSP 2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).

Results and discussion
33
After 50 years, a 75th percentile location reaches about 27.94 W/m (SSP 2-4.5), 
climbing to 28.39 W/m in SSP 5-8.5 (~1.8% increase). Over 100 years, the rates are 
31.52 W/m vs 31.71 W/m (only 0.6% higher in the high scenario). Under sustained 
operation for 100 years, these favorable locations achieve roughly 50.6 W/m with highemissions warming compared to 49.18 W/m with moderate warming (2.9% gain). 
Proportionally, this benefit is similar that seen in other percentile bands.
The consistent pattern across Figures 3.1-3.4 confirms that a warming climate yields 
a slight uplift in BHE thermal output across the board, irrespective of local yield class. 
Thus, by the year 2100, projected on the order of 2-3 W/m of additional capacity
scenario, for a given BHE and operational strategy.
Crucially, the rate of increase in heat extraction rate is not linear over time. In the 
initial decades of BHE operation, extraction rates were limited by the thermal 
drawdown around the borehole. However, as warming continues and the near-surface 
ground heat flux rises, the cooling of the ground by the BHE is increasingly offset by 
renewed heat inflow from the surface. In the model’s renewable scenario (where the 
BHE is operated such that it never drops below a safe minimum temperature over 50 
years), the long-term heat extraction rate eventually reaches a steady asymptote as 
ground warming replenishes the extracted heat. In a depleting scenario (where the 
BHE is pushed to exhaust a fixed thermal reservoir within 50 years), ground warming 
still contributes significantly to about a 1-6% higher cumulative extraction over 50 
years if an urban heat island or climate warming pre-exists for 100 years prior. This 
highlights that prior warming history can impart a lasting thermal memory benefit to 
geothermal systems (Benz et al., 2015).
The results thus reinforce that climate warming can delay or reduce the decline of BHE 
output over time. As ground temperatures rise, the borehole wall temperature remains 
higher for longer, allowing continued heat transfer to the circulating fluid. In practical 
terms, this means future borehole systems in Germany could maintain their heat 
output deeper into their operational life under warming conditions, compared to 
historical systems.
It is important to note, however, that the magnitude of these gains is relatively 
moderate and subject to uncertainty. While an increase of a few W/m in extraction rate 
per degree of ground warming is significant, it does not radically change the BHE 
design fundamentals. Additionally, the projections assume a uniform warming of the 
shallow subsurface according to climate models. Actual site-specific outcomes will 
depend on factors like soil moisture, thermal properties, and land cover change. For 
example, sites with higher soil water content may experience greater conductive heat 

Results and discussion
34
transfer and thus benefit slightly more from surface warming from the VDI 4640 (VDI, 
2010) directive studied by Ramos-Escudero & García-Cascales, (2022).
The findings in this chapter align with and extend the results of previous studies on 
shallow geothermal energy potential in the context of climate change. It is useful to 
compare the results with existing literature to validate the approach and highlight 
contributions. Several earlier works have investigated either the technical potential of 
GSHPs in specific regions or the impact of urbanization and climate on subsurface 
temperatures. Generally, the conclusions reinforce the consensus that shallow 
geothermal is a viable and significant renewable heat source in temperate regions, 
and that its potential is enhanced by factors like SUHIs effects and seasonal heat 
injection. 
Rivera et al. (2017) examined the effect of subsurface urban heat islands on 
geothermal potential in European cities. The results reported up to 40% higher 
technical potential in urban areas compared to rural areas and estimated that each 
degree of ground warming could save ~4 meters of borehole drilling. The nationwide 
analysis corroborates these specific figures in the German context. Similar magnitude 
increases were observed in urban settings, with 10-25% higher single BHE output in 
city centers, translating to approximately 4 m per °C in borehole length savings. This 
agreement with Rivera’s work lends credence to the model’s handling of urban heat 
effects. The findings align with that insight, as a ~1.4 °C higher ground warming in 
SSP 5-8.5 versus SSP 2-4.5 translates to a few percent higher extraction rate, echoing 
the notion that elevated subsurface temperatures increase shallow geothermal 
potential (Menberg et al., 2013).
Additionally, Benz et al. (2022) in Nature Communications introduced the concept of 
shallow subsurface heat recycling globally, concluding that warming ground could 
sustainably supply a significant share of space heating demand. The results provide 
a concrete case study backing that assertion. In Germany, by mid-century, a sizable 
fraction of building heating needs can be met by harnessing the warmed shallow 
ground, especially if integrated into broader systems. It was calculated that a single 
BHE might cover approximately 50% and up to 85% in optimized networks of a home’s 
heating demand by 2050 (Walch et al., 2022), aligning with the global estimate by 
Benz et al. (2022) that shallow geothermal could become a “sustainable global space 
heating alternative.”
Comparing to Walch et al. (2022), who focused on Switzerland and explored seasonal 
regeneration under climate scenarios, this study complements theirs by focusing on 
pure heating mode under climate change for Germany. Walch et al. (2022) found that 

Results and discussion
35
with combined heating-cooling operation, GSHPs could supply ~55% of heating and 
57% of cooling for individual buildings by 2050, rising to ~85% in district systems. In 
achieved results, similarly, find that individual GSHPs can supply more than half of a 
modern home’s heating load by mid-century, and if networked, a vast majority of 
thermal needs can be covered. A more heating dominant perspective was adopted, 
given Germany’s predominantly heating-driven climate. Nevertheless, the implications 
remain consistent shallow geothermal can support a substantial portion of the 
Wärmewende (heating transition). This comparison is encouraging, indicating the 
Germany focused analysis yields outcomes resonant with Swiss case studies, 
suggesting broader applicability in Central Europe. As also extend Walch’s work by 
providing a spatial dimension something their regional study did not detail, thereby 
identifying where within Germany this potential is highest.
Overall, it underscores a positive but incremental effect of climate change on shallow 
geothermal heat extraction over time, confirming that shallow geothermal systems will 
generally become more effective (for heating) as Germany’s climate warms which is 
a point echoed by recent studies highlighting climate-driven subsurface heat 
accumulation as an exploitable resource (Epting et al., 2018). At the same time, rising 
ground temperatures could diminish efficiency of geothermal cooling in summer 
(Schüppler et al., 2019) an aspect to consider for systems providing both heating and 
cooling.
3.2 Projected total usable power per BHEs
3.2.1 Mean and 50th percentile of power
The mean power capacity of a single borehole (150 m) increases with longer operation 
times and with sustainable use. Under the SSP 2-4.5 scenario, an average BHE can 
provide about 4045.57 W after 50 years of operation, increasing to 4657 W for a 100 
years operation, and up to 5503.03 W if operated sustainably with full thermal 
regeneration for 100 years (Figure 3.5). In the higher emission SSP 5-8.5 scenario, 
these mean outputs are slightly higher, roughly 4107.78 W (50 years), 4685.98 W (100 
years), and 5602.89 W (100 years sustainable).
The high-emission climate yields approximately 1-2% more power output per BHE 
compared to the mid-range environment, mirroring the per-meter trends. For instance, 
by 2100 a single BHE’s capacity is projected to increase by on the order of ~100 W 
due to the additional ground warming in SSP 5-8.5. In percentage terms, this is an 
uplift of ~1.8% in sustainable heat supply per borehole. Such an increase, while 
modest, could be significant when extrapolated across tens of thousands of 

Results and discussion
36
installations. Moreover, any increase in source temperature not only raises the heat 
output but also improves heat pump efficiency, leading to better COP for heating.
Figure 3.5: Graphical overview of mean projection for temporal power [W] for the SSP 2-4.5 & 
SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).
This means that a warmer ground will allow heat pumps to operate more efficiently, 
consuming less electricity for the same heat delivered, as has been noted in studies 
of GSHP performance under warming climates (Tissen et al., 2019).
Figure 3.6: Graphical overview of 50th percentile projection for temporal power [W] for the SSP 
2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).

Results and discussion
37
The median BHE power outputs (Figure 3.6) are very close to the mean values, 
indicating a symmetric distribution of site potentials without extreme outliers. After 50 
years, the median BHE yields around 4088.63 W in SSP 2-4.5 and 4151.94 W in SSP 
5-8.5. At 100 years, about 4680.28 W (SSP 2-4.5) to 4709.45 W (SSP 5-8.5) can be 
sustained, and under 100 years sustainable use, about 5573.12 W (SSP 2-4.5) to 
5674.63 W (SSP 5-8.5) is achievable per BHE. 
These numbers suggest that most areas in Germany can expect on the order of 5000-
6000 W of heat from a typical borehole by the end of the 2100, assuming standard 
depths on the order of 100-130 m. The minor (~2%) enhancement in median BHE 
power under SSP 5-8.5 again underscores that climate warming will slightly raise 
geothermal heating potential. This aligns with prior findings that elevated ground 
temperatures (whether from urban heat islands or global warming) tend to raise the 
geothermal technical potential by a measurable fraction (Zhu et al., 2015).
However, it is also evident that improvements due to climate are much smaller than 
those achieved by moving from 50 years to a sustainable 100 years operation. The 
latter yields ~20% increases in power (from ~4700 to ~5700 W) by avoiding long-term 
thermal depletion. This highlights that management of BHE operation (balanced heat 
extraction/reinjection) is more impactful than climate change in maximizing long-term 
output.
When contrasted with country-scale assessments such as the German Environment 
Agency’s scenario or Agora Energiewende’s targets, the findings provide a micro-level 
underpinning. Agora Energiewende has argued for increasing GSHPs installations 
from ~0.42 million today to about 3 million by 2050 (Hockenos, 2020). The UBA 
(Umweltbundesamt) scenarios even foresee shallow (plus deep) geothermal covering 
roughly 50% of Germany’s heat supply by 2050 in a study by published by Journalism 
for the energy transition (Hockenos, 2020).
The analysis gives technical evidence that supports these ambitions. The analysis 
demonstrates that the resource base (in terms of total W) is sufficient to achieve such 
penetration under climate warming conditions. If each BHE can provide slightly more 
output in the future (due to warmer ground and better utilization), then the number of 
BHEs needed to hit, say, 100 TWh of heat (which was one estimate for geothermal by 
2050) might be less than previously thought. Essentially, climate change might reduce 
the required drilling by a non-trivial percentage for the same heat output, making the 
2050 goals a bit easier to reach.
3.2.2 25th and 75th percentile of power

Results and discussion
38
Even for lower performing sites, the absolute power outputs are on the order of 
3994.42 W (50 years) to 4634.37 W (100 years) under current climate conditions (SSP 
2-4.5), rising to about 5421.32 W if operated sustainably for 100 years (Figure 3.7). 
Under the SSP 5-8.5 scenario, these increase to approximately 4036.73 W, 4655.26 
W, and 5489.58 W respectively. 
Figure 3.7: Graphical overview of 25th percentile projection for temporal power [W] for the SSP 
2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).
The fractional gains due to climate (~1% at 50 years, ~0.5% at 100 years, ~1.3% under 
sustainable 100 years) are slightly smaller in lower quartile locations, but still present. 
This means that even in geologically less favorable regions, future warming will 
marginally improve the potential output of shallow geothermal systems. The fact that 
a less favorable site's sustainable BHE can deliver over 5000 W of heat underscores 
that practically all regions have useful geothermal potential, even before accounting 
for any future technological improvements or system optimizations. 
It also emphasizes the importance of sustainable operation. Compare 50 years vs 
sustainable outputs (~4000 to 5400 W in SSP 2-4.5), which is an over 35% increase. 
This boost comes from allowing the ground to thermally recover each year (for 
example, via summertime passive recharge or balanced heating/cooling usage), 
preventing the long-term cooling that constrains output in continuous operation. The 
projected total usable power per BHE is on the scale of 4000-5400 W by 2100, with 
climate change providing a few percent bonus. This is a positive outcome for the 
feasibility of geothermal heat pumps, as it suggests a minor but tangible benefit from 
global warming in terms of resource capacity.

Results and discussion
39
The higher end expected capacities in more favorable conditions (e.g., areas with high 
ground thermal conductivity, aquifer driven convective heat transfer, or elevated 
subsurface temperatures). Under the SSP 2-4.5 climate scenario, higher-performing 
sites achieve power outputs of approximately 4191.67 W over 50 years, increasing to 
about 4727.65 W for a 100 years horizon, and reaching up to 5738.3 W when operated 
sustainably (Figure 3.8). In the warmer SSP 5-8.5 scenario, these capacities slightly 
improved, rising to around 4258.67 W (50 years), 4756.55 W (100 years), and 5844.19 
W (100 years sustainable operation).
Figure 3.8: Graphical overview of 75th percentile projection for temporal power [W] for the SSP 
2-4.5 & SSP 5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models).
The percentage gains from climate warming in these upper-quartile sites are 
noticeable but moderate, approximately 1.9% at 50 years, 1.1% at 100 years, and up 
to 2.5% under sustainable conditions. Such results emphasize that areas already 
benefiting from favorable geological and hydrogeological conditions will gain slightly 
more advantage from future subsurface warming. The nearly 5800 W potential from a 
single borehole under sustainable operation suggests a robust and significant 
resource, enough to easily satisfy heating demands of highly efficient residential 
buildings or contribute substantially to district heating schemes. The data also 
reinforces the importance of sustainable design practices, as sustainable operation 
yields roughly 42% greater heat output compared to continuous 50 years extraction 
scenarios.
These results indicate that Germany’s geothermal hotspots, which naturally achieve

Results and discussion
40
high extraction rates due to favorable geology, will see modest yet beneficial increases 
in power output under the higher emission SSP 5-8.5 climate scenario. This provides 
a positive outlook for geothermal development, underscoring that favorable sites could 
deliver even greater heat supply by 2100, enhancing the viability and attractiveness of 
shallow geothermal energy systems in future energy transition strategies.
Notably, borehole length can be traded off against ground temperature to achieve a 
given power. For instance, to continuously supply ~2 kW of heating (average demand) 
from one borehole, a depth of about 80 m is needed under baseline climate (ground 
surface temperature ~10 °C). However, with a +5 °C increase in GST due to urban 
heating or climate change, the required borehole length drops to only ~60 m. 
Prior studies caution that in areas with very high BHE densities, competition for ground 
heat can constrain output unless spacing is sufficient. In Switzerland, for instance, 
local authorities have set minimum borehole spacing to prevent over-exploitation of 
the shallow ground heat resource (SIA, 2010). In the projections for Germany’s 
technical potential, as assumed compliance with such spacing norms (following VDI 
4640 guidelines), thereby treating each BHE’s contribution independently. Under that 
assumption, total power scales roughly linearly with borehole length for a given ground 
condition. A 200 m BHE yields nearly double the heat of a 100 m BHE, etc., up to the 
point where deep ground temperatures approach undisturbed geothermal gradient 
conditions. However, diminishing returns can occur at great depths since the 
geothermal gradient (~0.03 K/m in Germany) adds only modestly warmer 
temperatures per additional meter. Climate change does not substantially alter this 
linear scaling, but it elevates the baseline of power.
In contrast, a pure heating-only operation might gradually cool the ground and slightly 
reduce the peak power over the years (though climate warming mitigates this). This 
seasonal regeneration significantly raises technical potential. Walch et al. (2022) found 
that annual heat extraction densities above 300 kWh/m² were achievable when 
roughly equal amounts of heat (~330 kWh/m²) were injected in summer from cooling 
loads. Thus, the total usable energy per BHE over a year could dramatically increase 
with higher cooling penetration scenario.
The results demonstrate that each individual BHE in Germany can provide a 
substantial and increasing amount of renewable thermal power under future climate 
conditions. On a per-unit basis, shallow geothermal systems will marginally improve 
their output W because of ground warming, helping to offset some effects of greater 
heating demand variability or colder design days. The results also hint at optimized 

Results and discussion
41
deployment. Shallow and intermediate depth boreholes become relatively more 
favorable as the climate warms, which could lower drilling costs per W of capacity. 
The result from sections 3.1 and 3.2 is the shows large difference between finite 50 or 
100 years or 100 years sustainable extraction scenarios. When the BHE is allowed to 
operate sustainably meaning thermal equilibrium is reached with annual recharge 
balancing extraction. The per-meter extraction rates and total power output are 
significantly higher (roughly 40-50% higher than the 100 year continuous operation in 
the results). This highlights a crucial point that is shallow geothermal energy can be 
renewable if managed correctly, but if operated too aggressively without enough heat 
replenishment time, the resource depletes (ground cools down), lowering the effective 
extraction rate over time. In this modeling, the 100 years sustainable scenario likely 
assumed that each year’s heat extraction was balanced (for example, by summer heat 
rejection or simply by cutting the extraction to align with natural heat flux). 
In practice, achieving true sustainability might involve integrating cooling uses or 
seasonal storage of heat (e.g., via solar thermal or ambient heat in summer) to 
recharge the ground. The payoff for doing so is evident is nearly 5.5-5.7 kW per 
borehole sustainably, versus ~4.6-4.7 kW if one were to run the system for 100 years 
straight without balance. Prior work underscores this as well as Rivera et al. (2017) 
note that shallower systems favor sustainable operation, and with the right conditions, 
one can avoid long-term temperature drift. Essentially, an unbalanced heating-only 
BHE is mining stored heat that took many years to accumulate, eventually it will cool 
the ground. But with intermittent or balanced use, the ground heat can renew annually 
and provide indefinite service.
The archived results quantify that the 50 years scenario can be thought of as an 
unbalanced extraction where after a few decades the ground temperature drop begins 
to constrain output. The fact that the 100 years unsustainable scenario had a higher 
rate than 50 years (due to climate warming compensation) should not mask that in 
absolute terms, continuous extraction without enough recharge will plateau and 
decline in the long run. 
The sustainable case shows the upper bound if that plateau is averted. It is 
encouraging that in a warming climate, the ground’s natural heat input is higher, 
meaning the threshold to achieve sustainability is a bit easier to meet (nature 
contributes a bit more heat). But even so, the design and operation of GSHP systems 
should strive for renewable operation. This can involve proper sizing (longer boreholes 
or more boreholes so that the heat flux from deep ground equals the extracted flux at 
steady state) and integrating heat rejection in summer (for example, using the heat 

Results and discussion
42
pump for cooling or dedicated regeneration). By doing so, not only is the long-term
performance improved, but higher initial extraction rates can be sustained without fear 
of excessive ground cooling (Tissen et al., 2019).
In Germany, guidelines such as the VDI 4640 (VDI 4640, 2010) and (VDI 4640, 2015) 
already account for sustainable heat extraction limits, often recommending design 
extraction rates (e.g. ~20-40 W/m) that ensure the ground temperature does not drop 
continuously year on year. In this study, the findings of ~46-50 W/m sustainable imply 
that, under certain conditions (likely to include seasonal recovery), one can achieve 
even higher rates in the long term. These values are within the realm reported by 
experimental and field studies for instance, an innovative BHE installation in Karlsruhe 
with enhanced aquifer flow showed possible extraction up to 100 W/m in active 
groundwater zones (Baden-Württemberg, 2005), and typical heat pump operations 
often cite ~50 W/m as a guideline peak for Central Europe. 
Thus, the sustainable scenario aligns with upper end real world performance where 
hydrogeological or operationally favorable conditions allow high renewable yields. The 
key takeaway is that system longevity and output are maximized when heat extraction 
is balanced with heat input. In the context of climate change, as winters become 
milder, it might become more feasible to achieve balance (since heating needs drop 
more than cooling needs rise studied by Olonscheck et al. (2011), or even to utilize 
excess summer heat (from air conditioning or waste heat) to inject into the ground, 
creating a virtuous cycle that increases winter capacities further.
3.3 Spatial distribution & variability across Germany
The Geographical distribution of maximum heat extraction rate [W/m] and power [W] 
per BHE across Germany for the SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5 scenarios is discussed in 
this section. The figure is composed of three maps: (a) 50 years operation, (b) 100
years operation, and (c) 100 years sustainable operation, highlighting regional 
variability in geothermal potential under a mid-range and high climate future.
3.3.1 Mean and Median (50th percentile) heat extraction rates [W/m]
Figures 3.9 (SSP 2-4.5) and 3.10 (SSP 5-8.5) illustrate the mean heat extraction rates 
across Germany for the 50 years, 100 years, and sustainable (100 years) operation 
periods. Under SSP 2-4.5, mean heat extraction rates range spatially from 
approximately 26 W/m in northern and eastern regions, up to around 46 W/m in 
southwestern and urban regions under sustainable condition. 

Results and discussion
43
Under SSP 5-8.5, these mean rates slightly increase, reaching up to 47 W/m, 
indicating a national average increment of about 2-4% due to increased climate 
forcing. Spatially, the highest mean extraction rates occur consistently in densely 
populated urban areas, including Berlin, Munich, Frankfurt, and the Rhine-Ruhr 
metropolitan region. These urban areas benefit significantly from anthropogenic heat 
inputs and favorable geological and hydrogeological conditions, enhancing their 
geothermal potential substantially.
Figure 3.9: Map of mean projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 2-4.5 (all 
CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
Figure 3.10: Map of mean projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 5-8.5
(all CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
Comparing these maps illustrates the spatial impact of stronger warming on the 
shallow geothermal potential by 2100. The maps in Figures 3.9 and 3.10 reveal 
pronounced spatial heterogeneity in shallow geothermal potential across Germany.

Results and discussion
44
Some regions consistently emerge as hotspots of higher heat extraction rates (yellow 
to light green areas), while others show more modest values (dark green to blue).
Figures 3.11 (SSP 2-4.5) and 3.12 (SSP 5-8.5) display the median heat extraction 
rates, representing typical geothermal potentials across Germany. Under SSP 2-4.5, 
median extraction rates are slightly lower than the mean, ranging from around 25 W/m 
in northern Germany to about 45 W/m under sustainable conditions in southern and 
urban areas. Under SSP 5-8.5, median rates are notably higher, typically around 48 
W/m sustainably. Geothermal hotspots appear in urbanized regions like Munich and 
Frankfurt and in the geothermal-rich southwestern area near the Rhine Valley, driven 
primarily by higher future warming conditions projected under SSP 5-8.5. 
Notably, in both scenarios, the North German Basin and the Upper Rhine Graben 
region stand out with elevated extraction potentials. These areas benefit from 
favorable geological conditions, for example, higher geothermal heat flow and thick 
sedimentary layers that store and conduct heat efficiently. The Upper Rhine Graben 
in southwest Germany is known for above-average geothermal gradients and has 
been a focus for deep geothermal projects (Sanjuan et al., 2022), which aligns with 
the high shallow heat extraction rates observed in that region. 
Figure 3.11: Map of 50th percentile projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 
2-4.5 (all CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
Similarly, northern German plains (Lower Saxony to Brandenburg) sit atop 
sedimentary basins with relatively higher terrestrial heat flow and shallower aquifers, 
contributing to better BHE performance. By contrast, parts of southern Bavaria 
(Molasse Basin) and some mountainous or upland areas show lower extraction rates 
(blueish or light tones). In alpine and upland regions, the geothermal gradient is often 

Results and discussion
45
lower and groundwater tables deeper, limiting heat replenishment from below (Weber 
et al., 2022).
Figure 3.12: Map of 50th percentile projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 
5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
Additionally, these areas have colder surface climates historically, which means lower 
initial ground temperatures, though this gap will partly close with global warming. 
Comparing SSP 5-8.5 maps to SSP 2-4.5 shows a subtle but discernible increase in 
extraction rates nationwide under the stronger warming scenario. Many regions shift 
towards warmer color shades, indicating a few extra W/m of potential. 
Crucially, the spatial patterns remain the same. Climate change uniformly boosts the 
baseline potential but does not create new hotspots or fundamentally alter which areas 
are best suited. For instance, under both scenarios, the highest extraction rates (over 
~30 [W/m] for 100 years unsustainable, and ~50 W/m for sustainable use) occur in the 
northwestern and southwestern parts of Germany. These can be interpreted as the 
areas where shallow geothermal can be most effectively harnessed, possibly 
supplying a larger share of heating needs locally. 
Indeed, it is no coincidence that many existing geothermal heating installations and 
district heating plants in Germany are concentrated in those favorable regions 
(Molasse Basin in the south, North German Basin, and Rhine Graben) (GeotIS, 2009 
from Weber et al., 2022).
