Figures 3.15 (SSP 2-4.5) and 3.16 (SSP 5-8.5) present the 75th percentile heat 
extraction rates, highlighting regions with particularly favorable subsurface conditions. 

Results and discussion
47
Under SSP 2-4.5, rates span approximately 27 W/m to about 47 W/m sustainably. 
Notably high geothermal potentials are found in urban regions like Berlin, Frankfurt, 
and the Rhine-Ruhr metropolitan area, as well as geologically favorable areas in the 
southwest. Under SSP 5-8.5, these optimal locations achieve sustainable rates of
around 48 W/m, illustrating how enhanced climate warming can further boost 
geothermal potential in already favorable areas by about 2-5%.
The spatial variability shown by the percentile analysis and maps underscores that 
local subsurface properties are a dominant factor in geothermal potential. Thermal 
conductivity, undisturbed ground temperature, groundwater flow, and geology vary 
across Germany, leading to the range of values (the difference between, say, 25th and 
75th percentiles in Figures 3.13-3.16). For example, areas with significant groundwater 
flow (e.g. in permeable aquifers along river plains) can sustain higher heat extraction 
due to advection replenishing heat field experiments have found up to ~13% 
improvement in heat transfer in aquifers sediments (Wang et al., 2009). 
Field experiments and modeling studies have found that even a modest groundwater 
flow (on the order of a few cm per day) can boost the total energy yield by on the order 
of 10-20% (Piipponen et al., 2024). In aquifer sediments, flowing groundwater 
effectively increases the apparent thermal conductivity by up to ~10–15%, thereby 
enhancing heat transfer rates and delaying ground cooling around the borehole
(Gehlin & Hellström, 2003). 
Figure 3.15: Map of 75th percentile projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 
2-4.5 (all CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
In the national scale results, large cities likely contribute to some localized high values, 
although the scenario reflects climate-driven changes (urban anthropogenic heat
inputs were the focus). Nevertheless, the principle is the same, warmer ground means 

Results and discussion
48
greater geothermal technical potential. It is important to note that while climate change 
modestly increases the potential everywhere, the spatial ranking of regions by 
geothermal productivity remains consistent. 
Figure 3.16: Map of 75th percentile projection for spatial heat extraction rate [W/m] for the SSP 
5-8.5 (all CMIP6 models) (a) for 50 years, (b) for 100 years and (c) for 100 years sustainable.
Schiel et al. (2016) studied Ludwigsburg (a city in southern Germany) and concluded 
that a significant fraction of urban heating demand could be met by BHE fields, though 
careful spacing and accounting for urban heat was needed. The spatial maps 
generalize such city scale findings. For instance, results in maps would classify 
Ludwigsburg’s area as having good potential (as it lies in a sedimentary basin with 
moderate SUHI effect). The implication is that what was found possible in isolated 
cases (like Ludwigsburg) likely holds across many German cities, a notion the 
nationwide approach confirms. Likewise, regional mapping efforts in Bavaria 
(Bertermann et al., 2024) Mapping very shallow geothermal potential has shown that 
even rural areas have decent geothermal potential, which the maps echo for the whole 
country.
Therefore, strategic planning for geothermal deployment should prioritize naturally 
favorable regions (as identified in these maps) for large-scale projects, while also 
recognizing that even less favorable regions will improve slightly and should not be 
dismissed for smaller scale heat pump use. The nationwide rise in shallow 
groundwater temperatures (projected ~+2 to 3.5 °C) will effectively lift. By 2100, many 
locations in Germany could see their shallow subsurface temperatures approach or 
exceed 12-15 °C, especially in the high scenario. This general warming trend bodes 
well for the wider adoption of GSHPs, since higher source temperatures improve 
system efficiency and output. Indeed, warmer ground might allow shorter boreholes or 

Results and discussion
49
reduced drilling needs for the same heating capacity, potentially lowering installation 
costs or improving the economic feasibility of GSHP systems in marginal areas.
