Several factors drive this variability such as geological conditions (thermal conductivity 
and groundwater presence), regional climate differences, and urbanization effects. 
The analysis reveals distinct patterns. Broadly, areas with high groundwater table and 
conductive unconsolidated sediments (such as parts of the North German Plain and 
Upper Rhine Graben) show higher shallow geothermal potentials, whereas areas with 
dry, insulative soils or hard rock near the surface yield lower potential.
One critical determinant is the thermal conductivity and moisture content of the 
subsurface. According to the German VDI 4640 standard, water-saturated sandy or 
gravelly soils can have extractable heat rates on the order of 50-70 W/m, whereas the 
same soils in unsaturated conditions might support 30-40 W/m (VDI 4640 Directive).

Results and discussion
53
The mapped results reflect these regions with abundant groundwater (e.g. lowland 
plains, river valleys) dark colored with higher extraction rates per meter. For example, 
much of northern Germany, with its quaternary aquifers, appears as a hotspot for 
shallow geothermal. Here, even a 50 m BHE can be quite productive due to saturated 
sediments, a finding supported by regional studies mapping Bavaria and other states. 
By contrast, upland areas with shallow bedrock or dry soils (such as parts of the 
Central German highlands and some Alpine foothills) exhibit lower potential. 
In these zones, the maps indicate perhaps 20-25 W/m extraction rates, requiring 
deeper drilling or more boreholes to achieve the same output as in groundwater-rich 
areas. This pattern underscores the importance of hydrogeology like the presence of 
groundwater not only boosts conduction but also can induce some convective heat 
transport, effectively increasing the renewable heat recharge around the borehole 
(Richter et al., 2024).
Regional climate differences also imprint on spatial variability. Southern Germany 
presently has slightly warmer mean ground temperatures (~11-12 °C annual average 
in the Rhine valley) compared to the north (~9-10 °C in some northern plains). Under 
climate change, these differences persist or grow. The climate model inputs suggest 
coastal Northern Germany might warm somewhat less than inland southern regions 
by late century. Consequently, slightly higher extraction rates are projected in the 
south and west (where both geology and warming may be favorable) relative to the 
north/east. However, the contrast due to climate alone is relatively minor (on the order 
of 1-2 W/m difference) compared to geological factors. The maps show a tapestry of 
potential that does not align neatly with a north-south gradient, indicating geology 
dominates. 
Some regions have been well characterized (e.g. Bavaria has detailed maps 
(Bertermann et al., 2024), and the GeotIS database compiles thousands of borehole 
records for many areas) whereas others (perhaps parts of former East Germany or 
less developed rural areas) lack dense data. In the modeling, interpolated or assumed 
values for poorly sampled areas, which introduces uncertainty.
Another layer of spatial variability comes from urban vs. rural land use. The results 
incorporate the effect of urban heat islands (UHIs) on subsurface temperatures where 
applicable. Major cities in Germany (Berlin, Hamburg, Munich, Cologne, etc.) tend to 
have elevated ground surface temperatures due to paving, basements, and waste 
heat, which leads to locally higher ground temperatures (often 2-5 °C above rural 
surroundings). As a result, city centers often show up as having higher geothermal 
potential per BHE than their rural hinterlands. For example, the map for heat extraction 

Results and discussion
54
rates likely shows a patch of higher values in the Cologne metropolitan area, 
consistent with observations of +2-4 °C subsurface warming there. Similarly, Berlin’s 
inner city might exhibit a boost. This is in line with findings by Menberg et al. (2013)
and Zhu et al. (2010), who measured significantly warmer ground in German city 
centers and linked it to increased technical potential. 
It’s worth noting that the spatial maps integrate both renewable and depleting
perspectives of geothermal use. Areas of high potential on the map indicate that even 
under a sustainable operation (renewable scenario), a substantial heat flux can be 
drawn without long-term exhaustion of the resource, due to surface recharge or ground 
storage. In contrast, areas of low potential would reach the renewable limit much 
sooner, meaning any attempt to extract beyond that would lead to quick temperature 
decline. For example, an area mapped with 20 W/m might support that extraction for 
decades if operated renewably, but trying to extract 40 W/m there would rapidly 
deplete the ground heat within a few years.
The spatial analysis suggests that renewable extraction rates (rates that can be 
sustained indefinitely) are often about 50-70% of the maximum “depleting” rates. 
Regions with high natural replenishment (e.g. high rainfall infiltration, urban waste heat 
inputs) have renewable rates closer to their maximum. These dynamics underpin the 
color scales in Figure 3.23 and 3.24, which likely depicts projected usable power per 
BHE across Germany, essentially combining the rate per meter with typical depths 
shows how many W each BHE could supply in different locations. In that figure, one 
can observe how certain pockets (e.g. Alpine foreland aquifers, parts of the Rhine rift, 
North Sea coastal plains) have very high BHE outputs, whereas some hard-rock 
regions (parts of the Black Forest, Erzgebirge, etc.) are down per BHE. This spread 
reflects the interplay of geology and climate and highlights that geothermal feasibility 
is location specific.
The spatial analysis of shallow geothermal potential in Germany under climate change 
shows a heterogeneous yet promising landscape. Climate warming generally lifts all 
boats (i.e. increases potential everywhere), but inherent geological differences lead to 
a patchwork of higher vs. lower potential areas. The highest potential coincides with 
populated plains and valleys, which is fortunate for utilization, while lower potentials in 
mountainous areas may limit geothermal to a supporting role. These findings will 
inform regional planning. 
The spatial analysis in section 3.3 showed that not all regions are equal in geothermal 
potential. Geology and hydrogeology are critical determinants. Areas in northern and 
southern Germany differ in stratigraphy, the North German Basin is filled with thick 

Results and discussion
55
sedimentary sequences (sandstones, clays, etc.) with relatively high porosity and often 
near-surface aquifers, whereas the South German Molasse basin and crystalline 
regions have different characteristics. These differences manifest in thermal 
properties that the sediments saturated with groundwater can have high effective 
thermal conductivity and also benefit from convective heat transport, both of which 
enhance heat extraction rates (Krämer, 2010). 
In contrast, dry or crystalline rocks have lower thermal conductivity and lack convective 
heat transfer, limiting heat recharge to a BHE. Furthermore, background heat flow 
varies. Studies have shown that Germany’s heat flow ranges roughly from ~50-60 
mW/m² in some parts of Bavaria up to ~100 mW/m² in the Rhine Graben, with an 
average ~20% higher than previously assumed due to updated data (Fuchs et al., 
2022). A higher geothermal heat flow means more natural heat is available from depth, 
which directly supports higher sustainable extraction (it’s essentially the per square 
meter supply of heat from below). Therefore, regions with higher heat flow (e.g., the 
Rhine Graben, northwestern Germany) inherently allow more heat to be pumped out 
sustainably, this is evident in resulted maps where those regions yield the warmest 
colors for the sustainable scenario. 
Aquifer presence is another regional factor. In northern Germany, shallow 
groundwater is ubiquitous and often moving, this can substantially increase local 
geothermal potential by carrying heat laterally. As noted, even moderate groundwater 
flux can boost extraction by >10% studied by Wang et al. (2009). Conversely, in 
regions with deep water tables or little groundwater movement (e.g., some hilly central 
regions or highlands), BHEs rely purely on conduction, which is a slower heat transfer 
mode. This is reflected in the lower quartile of sites which likely correspond to such 
conditions. 
Additionally, urbanization can create micro-regional anomalies such major cities 
(Berlin, Hamburg, Munich, Frankfurt, Stuttgart etc.) are not resolved explicitly in the 
country-scale model, but real-world data show groundwater under cities is significantly
warmer (by several degrees) studied by Menberg et al. (2012). If one zoomed into 
those city areas, the potential per BHE would be higher than the surrounding rural 
average for example, Menberg et al. (2013) documented urban groundwater in 
Cologne ~3-5 °C warmer than rural areas, which implies a considerable increase in 
heat potential. The median and percentile results, which aggregate across large areas, 
implicitly include both urban and rural locations. This is one reason the mean and 
median were slightly below the 75th percentile (since a few urban hotspots likely push 
the 75th percentile up). 

Results and discussion
56
For energy planning, these regional differences mean that a one size fits all approach 
to geothermal deployment is suboptimal. Instead, identifying and focusing on highpotential regions could yield disproportionate benefits. For instance, community 
geothermal heating projects (like district heating networks using shallow geothermal) 
might be most viable in northern lowlands and the southwest, where fewer boreholes 
can supply a given load. In lower-potential regions, individual heat pumps for single 
homes may still work efficiently (especially with reduced demand and some assistance 
from climate warming), but large-scale projects might require either deeper drilling or 
hybrid systems to meet big loads. The German government and researchers 
recognize this geothermal development roadmaps often highlight the Molasse Basin, 
North German Basin, and Rhine Graben as key areas (Suchi et al., 2014), which 
coincides with these findings. Nevertheless, no region is entirely unviable, even the 
coolest areas in the maps delivered ~25–30 W/m (100 years sustainable), which for a 
standard 150 m BHE is ~2.5-3 kW, enough to significantly contribute to a household’s 
heating. As climate warms, these “cool” areas will also gain a bit of ground 
temperature, raising their yields slightly.
3.4. Uncertainties, limitations and enhancements
While the results above paint an encouraging picture for shallow geothermal energy 
under climate change, it is essential to acknowledge the uncertainties and 
assumptions inherent in the analysis. This subsection discusses the key sources of 
uncertainty, the sensitivity of outcomes to various parameters, and the limitations of 
the modeling approach. The goal is to transparently address the error bars on these 
projections and identify which factors could significantly alter the conclusions if varied. 
The quantitative results have error bars are roughly ±20% on extraction rates 
regionally, perhaps ±0.5 kW on per BHE power, and maybe ±5-10 percentage points 
on the fraction of heating demand a single BHE can cover. These uncertainties are 
not negligible, but they do not undermine the feasibility of shallow geothermal rather, 
they guide where caution or further research is needed. For instance, in regions where 
the map shows borderline viability (~15 W/m), the lower bound of uncertainty might 
drop that to ~10 W/m, suggesting those areas should be assessed carefully (or 
combined with supplemental heat). Conversely, high potential areas may be even 
better than if more data shows higher saturation or if cooling regeneration is utilized.
3.4.1. Uncertainties and model limitation
Climate projection: A specific climate scenario (medium-high emissions) was used to 
drive ground temperature changes (Eyring et al., 2016). Currently, warming could be 

Results and discussion
57
lower or higher. As noted, shallow groundwater warming by 2100 could range from 
roughly 2.1 °C to 3.0 °C on average depending on emissions pathways studied by 
Benz et al. (2024) and another researcher. This range translates into a corresponding 
uncertainty in heat extraction rates. If warming is on the low end, the boost in BHE 
performance would be smaller (perhaps only a 5-10% gain instead of ~15% by 
century’s end). Conversely, a high-emission future might further increase shallow 
geothermal output (potentially more than 20% gains in some regions). Greater 
confidence is placed in near-term projections; beyond mid-century, policy choices and 
global emissions introduce increasing divergence. This is partially addressed by 
focusing on the general trend rather than exact values for 2100. 
However, planners should be aware that climate outcomes outside the assumed 
scenario will shift the results accordingly. One mitigation is to incorporate multiple 
climate scenarios in future studies e.g. analyzing both a low-emission and a highemission case to bound the possibilities. The single scenario approach is a 
simplification that could be refined in future modeling. 
Subsurface thermal properties: The subsurface properties (thermal conductivity, heat 
capacity, presence of groundwater) in each location are imperfectly known. Relied on 
geological maps and VDI 4640 standard values, but there is natural heterogeneity. 
Even within a small area, soil layers, moisture, and rock types vary. This introduces 
uncertainty in the magnitude of extraction rates. A sensitivity analysis reveals that 
among design parameters, the initial ground temperature and the fraction of the 
borehole in saturated ground are among the top influences on BHE performance 
(Richter et al., 2024). For example, if the true thermal conductivity in a region is 20% 
lower than assumed, the model would overestimate the extraction rate by roughly a 
similar fraction. Nonetheless, local deviations can be significant. Thermal response 
tests (TRTs) at specific sites often show that actual performance can be ±10-30% 
different from generic estimates due to local geology (Focaccia, 2013). In the 
projections at a national scale, this manifests as uncertainty bands on the maps that 
some areas could be better or worse than depicted. 
To improve confidence, more site-specific measurements (e.g. TRT data, borehole 
logs) should be integrated. Unfortunately, such data are sparse in some regions, as 
Ramos-Escudero and García-Cascales (2022) note, the lack of detailed subsurface 
data remains a barrier, especially outside well-studied regions. The scenarios suggest 
that future work incorporates probabilistic approaches. Instead of a single value, 
assign distributions to thermal parameters and perform Monte Carlo simulations. This 
would yield error bars on the expected extraction rates. Initial steps in this direction 

Results and discussion
58
(e.g. global sensitivity and uncertainty quantification studies) show it’s feasible to 
quantify the probability of outcomes like borehole under sizing or temperature limit 
exceedance (Watanabe et al., 2010). 
Groundwater flow and advection: A major assumption in the model is that heat 
transport in the ground is predominantly conductive (except for anthropogenic vertical 
heat flux in urban areas). Regional groundwater flow (advection), which can carry heat 
and alter subsurface temperatures, was not explicitly modeled. In aquifer-rich areas, 
groundwater movement can enhance heat replenishment to boreholes by bringing in 
warmer water from elsewhere (or conversely, carry heat away). This can be a doubleedged sword, in some cases, flowing groundwater dramatically increases the 
sustainable heat extraction (by effectively increasing the thermal conductivity), in other 
cases, very cold groundwater flow could cool an area and reduce output (Taylor et al., 
2009). Generally, in Germany, many shallow aquifers have slow-moving groundwater, 
so conduction dominates at the scale of a single BHE. But localized effects (e.g. near 
rivers or in karst systems) could be significant. 
By excluding advective heat transport, this model might underestimate potential in 
some high-flow scenarios (absence of the extra heat supply) or fail to catch a limitation 
in others. The sensitivity analysis by Gunawardhana et al. (2015) indeed identified the 
presence of groundwater flow as a critical factor, if a large portion of the borehole is 
influenced by flowing water, the uncertainty and performance impact is substantial. 
Future modeling enhancements (as discussed in section 3.7) should incorporate 
hydrogeological models or at least scenario bracketing (no-flow vs. high-flow) to see 
how results change. The current nationwide assessment likely errors on the 
conservative side by neglecting positive contributions of advection in most aquifers. 
Borehole thermal interference: Adequately spaced BHEs were assumed to prevent 
thermal interference. If many systems are installed in proximity (e.g. a dense borehole 
field supplying a neighborhood), the extraction rates per BHE could decline over time 
as their thermal plumes overlap. This is a model assumption that could lead to an 
overestimation of aggregate potential if not managed. However, the German 
guidelines (and the approach) inherently address this by specifying minimum spacing 
(often on the order of 5-6 m or more between boreholes). Also, the concept of an 
infinite field of BHEs was considered by Rivera et al. (2017), who found that even in 
extreme coverage (infinite field scenario), renewable extraction can supply on the 
order of 4-16% of urban heating demand sustainably, and up to ~40% under depleting 
use with deep boreholes. These numbers give confidence that even if multiple BHEs 
interact, the overall system can still meet a meaningful fraction of demand. 

Results and discussion
59
To be cautious, policymakers should implement thermal licensing practices ensuring 
new boreholes are spaced and rated such that they do not encroach on each other’s 
thermal radius. The model, by focusing on single BHE metrics, effectively requires 
separate post-processing to scale to multiple systems. Uncertainty arises when 
scaling if one tries to cover 100% of an area with BHEs at max output, the single BHE 
results won’t directly apply due to interference. This is not a flaw, but a scope choice 
of the uncertainty is not in the single BHE performance, but in how to extrapolate it to 
high-density deployment.
Heating demand evolution and usage patterns: The discussion so far implicitly 
assumes a certain heating demand profile (based on current practices and moderate 
improvements in efficiency). But a lot can change by 2050 means buildings are 
expected to become more energy-efficient, and heating demand may decrease overall 
in Germany due to insulation retrofits and warmer winters. Conversely, cooling 
demand is likely to increase in summer (Möller et al., 2018). These socio-technical 
factors introduce uncertainty in how the geothermal systems will be used, which feeds 
back into performance. For instance, if winter heating demand drops significantly (due 
to efficiency gains) while summer cooling demand rises (due to hotter summers and 
more AC adoption), then many GSHP systems will operate in a balanced or even heatinjection-dominated mode. This would greatly improve ground thermal conditions for 
extraction (due to seasonal regeneration) (Werner, 2016). 
The model did not explicitly simulate different demand scenarios as it is essentially 
assumed to be a heating-dominated operation with some seasonal recovery (typical 
of present-day usage). If instead a future scenario entails heavy cooling usage, the 
current extraction projections might be understated to those BHEs could potentially 
yield more heat annually because they get recharged in summer. On the other hand, 
if heat pumps are undersized or used flexibly with smart grids, the load profile might 
shift, which could slightly reduce thermal stress on the ground and improve 
sustainability. 
Model abstraction and neglected processes: A relatively simplified semi-analytical 
model framework (based on moving finite line source theory and GIS data) was used. 
This necessarily involved assumptions like homogeneous ground layers, no explicit 
time-dependent heat pump COP variation, etc. Some neglected processes that could 
introduce errors include its freeze/thaw effects in very shallow ground (if a heat pump 
extracts a lot, the ground could freeze, altering thermal properties), thermo-mechanical 
effects (ground heat or borehole grout property changes with temperature), and heat 
pump performance degradation in extreme climate conditions. These are generally 

Results and discussion
60
second-order effects for the scope, but they contribute to the error margin (Walch et 
al., 2012). For example, if extreme cold spells occur less often in the future, heat 
pumps will run more efficiently on average (improving the effective heat delivery of the 
system). Hourly variations and COP were not explicitly modeled; incorporating them 
could refine the usable heat estimates likely upward, given that future milder winters 
imply higher COP. On the flip side, extreme heat waves might reduce cooling COP 
when ground is warm, a factor considered cooling supply. While these are beyond the 
resolution of the study, it’s important to state that real-world performance can deviate 
from idealized models. 
Field monitoring of existing GSHP installations often shows slightly lower output than 
theoretical due to system losses and operational issues. It is assuming well-designed 
systems if systems are mis-sized or poorly controlled, outcomes differ. Thus, there is 
an implicit uncertainty of a few percent due to system integration issues. However, 
those are engineering optimizations that can be improved with experience. In tackling 
uncertainties, one strategy is to examine extreme bounding cases e.g., what if the
ground is much less conductive than thought? Or what if climate warms more slowly? 
These sensitivity checks generally show the central conclusions and the qualitative 
findings (ground warming helps, spatial trends follow geology, etc.) hold true even 
under parameter variations. 
Policy decisions can also influence these outcomes, which is not a traditional model 
uncertainty but still an important assumption. The scenario presumed no major sociopolitical disruptions to deploying BHEs (aside from technical limits). If regulations were 
to overly restrict geothermal drilling (due to seismic fears or competing land use), then 
even high technical potential might not be realized. Conversely, strong incentives and 
R&D could overcome some limitations (e.g., new drill technologies reducing cost could 
allow deeper BHEs where needed, mitigating geological constraints). The realization 
of the projected potential is contingent on supportive frameworks. Thus, uncertainty is 
whether the current barriers (cost, awareness, regulatory) are removed or not. The 
results assume gradually improving uptake consistent with climate targets, but if 
uptake falters, the impact of shallow geothermal on Germany’s energy system could 
be less than technically possible. 
The reduced heating demand in buildings in a warm climate. Studies project that space 
heating needs in Germany will decrease substantially by mid-century and beyond 
under all climate scenarios (Olonscheck et al., 2011). Combined with improvements 
in building insulation and efficiency, the overall demand could drop significantly. This 
means that even if each BHE provides only slightly more heat, the fraction of building 

Results and discussion
61
heating demand that a given BHE can cover will likely increase. In other words, climate 
change makes both the supply side (ground heat) a bit more plentiful and the demand 
side (heating load) smaller. This convergence is beneficial for achieving heating selfsufficiency with heat pumps.
It emphasizes that the evaluation, while based on the best available data and methods, 
comes with a range of uncertainty. Climate variability, subsurface heterogeneity, 
demand changes, and modeling simplifications each introduce a margin of error. Key 
messages are “ground warming increases output” and “geology drives spatial 
differences”. However, the exact numbers should be treated as estimates rather than 
precise predictions. Ongoing field measurements, model refinements, and scenario 
analyses will be critical to narrow these uncertainties.
In summary, behavioral and technological uncertainties (building efficiency, adoption 
of cooling, demand management) cloud the exact future capacity factors of these 
systems. As addressed, this qualitatively outlined by Walch et al. (2022) scenarios, 
but a more rigorous treatment would include multiple demand cases. The uncertainty 
here is not so much about physics but about human factors and policy. As a result, the 
estimates for the fraction of demand covered (e.g. 50% by one BHE) could vary. If 
buildings use less heat, that fraction goes up (good for geothermal), if many add 
cooling, the fraction could also go up due to regeneration. These uncertainties mostly 
suggest that the projections are conservative in terms of geothermal role that there 
are plausible futures where shallow geothermal performs even better relative to needs, 
particularly if integrated into efficient, flexible energy systems.
While this study provides a comprehensive analysis of shallow geothermal extraction 
under future climates, there are several limitations to acknowledge. 
This modeling assumes a certain representative borehole length and configuration, 
and the results (in W/m and W) are averaged or percentile-based across broad 
regions. Individual installations will vary, a deeper borehole would yield more total 
power (approximately linearly with length until reaching diminishing returns due to 
increasing temperature with depth), and multiple boreholes (BHE fields) could be used 
for larger demands. As explicitly simulate borehole fields or the thermal interference 
between closely spaced BHEs. Future research could extend this work by examining 
urban-scale deployments with many BHEs operating in proximity, to see how climate 
warming vs. intra-field thermal interactions plays out. 
Groundwater flow and heterogeneity were only implicitly considered via the percentile 
spread. A more detailed hydrogeological model would be needed to quantify how 

Results and discussion
62
specific aquifer systems (e.g., in the North German Basin) enhance extraction under 
climate change. Groundwater not only increases heat transport but also has its own 
response to climate (e.g., changes in recharge patterns and temperatures). The use 
of scenarios SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5 for temperature presumably did not fully 
account for changes in groundwater levels or flow due to climate. If climate change 
leads to significantly drier conditions or shifts in groundwater tables, that could impact 
geothermal systems (for instance, a drop in water table could reduce the effective 
thermal conductivity of near-surface soils by drying them). Studies indicate climate 
change could affect groundwater levels in parts of Germany by 2100 studied by 
Wunsch et al. (2022), which in turn might affect heat transport. This aspect was outside 
the scope but warrants attention in future coupled hydro-thermal climate impact 
models. 
Climate forcing data was taken from scenarios that provide likely ranges of warming. 
A specific trajectory was assumed essentially end of century conditions for two 
scenarios. However, uncertainty remains regarding the magnitude of regional ground 
warming, as it depends on factors such as soil properties, land use changes, and 
variations in snow cover. Empirical observations already show warming of shallow 
ground and groundwater in many locations, on the order of 0.1 K per year in some 
urban settings outlined by Schweighofer et al. (2021) and detectable trends in rural 
settings as well (Menberg et al., 2014). 
Continued monitoring is needed to validate these projections. There is also the 
question of how deep the influence of surface warming will penetrate by 2100. The
analysis implicitly considered the upper ~150 m to be affected. Thermal diffusion 
means that at 150 m depth the temperature increase might be slightly lagged or 
attenuated compared to the surface. The depth profile evolution was not explicitly 
modeled, representing an area for further refinement. Numerical heat transport models 
(e.g., using the Fourier equation with surface boundary forcing) could be employed to 
simulate ground temperature profiles over time. These have shown that at depths of 
150 m, the ground might warm a bit less than the surface or with a delay of a few 
decades (Menberg et al., 2014). If so, the estimates for the 100 years scenario might 
be a bit optimistic in assuming the full warming applies throughout the borehole. A 
treatment might slightly reduce the climate benefit, but likely not by a large degree 
(since even partial warming of the top 50 m would help significantly). 
3.4.2. Future enhancements
High resolution hydrothermal modeling: One clear avenue is the development of 
models that couple groundwater flow with heat transport in the shallow subsurface. 

Results and discussion
63
This study (like many before it) primarily used conductive heat transport assumptions. 
Incorporating advection in aquifers through high-resolution numerical models (e.g., 3D 
finite element groundwater models) would allow to simulate how moving groundwater 
spreads heat around boreholes over time. This is especially important for locals with 
significant horizontal groundwater flow (such as valley filling aquifers or coastal 
aquifers). A future model could answer questions like Does a pumping well or natural 
gradient carry away the heat front from a borehole, thereby increasing sustainable 
extraction? Or does it bring cold water that cools the borehole? These details matter 
for optimizing systems. Moreover, with climate change, groundwater levels and flow 
patterns might shift (due to changing recharge), which could be captured in a coupled 
climate groundwater geothermal model (Smerdon & Pollack, 2016). There have been 
recent attempts at continental-scale modeling (Benz et al., 2024) but downscaling that 
to country or city scale with detailed hydrogeology would significantly enhance local 
predictions. 
Global sensitivity and uncertainty analysis: Building on the work of Richter et al. (2024) 
who applied machine learning assisted sensitivity analysis, future efforts should 
incorporate uncertainty quantification directly into shallow geothermal potential 
studies. For example, instead of outputting a single map of extraction rates, one could 
output a map of 90% confidence intervals of those rates. This would require 
propagating uncertainties in input parameters (thermal conductivity, surface 
temperature change, etc.) through the model. Techniques like Monte Carlo simulation 
or polynomial chaos expansions can be employed (Stone, 2011). The result would be 
more informative for decision-makers so that they’d see not just the best estimate but 
also the range of possible outcomes (e.g., an area might have 30 W/m ± 5 W/m at 
95% confidence). Richter et al. (2024) identified which parameters cause the largest 
output variance (initial ground temp, heat demand, groundwater influence), future 
modeling should measure and reduce these uncertainties. For instance, if the initial 
ground temperature is highly influential, better mapping of current ground temps (more 
borehole logging). If heat demand profiles influence outcomes, they incorporate 
multiple demand scenarios rather than a single average. The result of this work would 
be to improve robustness of geothermal potential studies, making them more bankable 
for investors and planners. 
Dynamic climate use scenarios: This study considered climate warming impacts in a 
quasi-static way (present vs future). A more sophisticated approach is to run transient 
simulations over decades, simultaneously evolving climate and the operation of the 
heat pump (Dick et al., 2013). This would capture feedback, like how gradually rising 
GST interplay with gradually improving heat pump efficiency and possibly changing

Results and discussion
64
user behavior. It would also allow exploring extremes or abrupt changes, e.g., what if 
a decade is much warmer or colder than average, how does the system respond? Or 
if an extreme drought lowers the water table significantly (reducing saturation), how 
does that affect output? Incorporating outputs from ensembles of climate models (with 
variability and extremes) would give a fuller picture of performance over time, not just 
endpoints. Furthermore, scenario analysis on market penetration of cooling could be 
integrated. Imagine running one model where 0% of buildings have cooling (heat-only 
GSHPs), and another where 50% have adopted cooling by mid-century (introducing 
seasonal heat injection). Comparing these would quantify the benefit of seasonal 
regeneration under climate warming something Walch et al. (2021) did at a high level, 
but which could be done spatially for Germany. These dynamic models could help 
answer, for instance by what year might a typical borehole start to see an increase 
(instead of decrease) in its winter heat output due to accumulated climate warming? 
Early in operation it might drop (ground cooling effect), but after some years of 
warming, it levels or increases. Quantifying that time horizon for various emissions 
scenarios would be academically and practically useful. 
Integration of real-world performance data: There is a growing body of measured 
performance data from operating GSHP systems (COPs, extracted energy, ground 
temperature evolution). Future modeling should assimilate this data to calibrate and 
validate these predictions. For example, monitored GSHP installations in Germany 
over more than 10 years can show how their output changed and whether the modeled 
trends hold. Did systems installed in 2010 extract slightly more energy by 2020 
because the ground warmed by ~0.5 K in that period? Gathering and analyzing such 
data would be valuable exercise. 
It addresses a data gap while borehole logs and lab measurements, long-term 
operational data is not systematically collected or shared. Creating a national 
database of GSHP performance (anonymized but with location and geology) would 
allow a cross comparison with model outputs. The GeotIS database in Germany 
collects geological data showed by Ramos-Escudero & García-Cascales (2022) but 
perhaps expanding it to include operational stats from heat pump owners (via incentive 
programs or reporting requirements) could vastly improve modeling inputs like typical 
load profiles and effective thermal properties. 
Machine learning and mapping: Modern data science offers tools like machine learning 
to map geothermal potential using heterogeneous data sources. One approach being 
tested by Assouline et al. (2019) is using machine learning to combine geological 
maps, surface temperatures, land use, etc., to predict ground temperature and thermal 

Results and discussion
65
conductivity in 3D. This can fill in data gaps in regions with sparse borehole data by 
learning patterns from regions with good data. For example, a model could learn the 
relationship between geology, elevation, rainfall, and measured ground temperature 
at 20 m depth from hundreds of boreholes, then predict it elsewhere. The use of such 
techniques is recommended to continuously update and refine spatial models. 
Additionally, remote sensing data such as satellite derived land surface temperature 
trends or nighttime light data as a proxy for urban heat input, as used by Gorelick et 
al. (2017) can be incorporated to improve urban ground temperature estimates. Benz 
et al. (2015) used global datasets in a similar way. For Germany, high-resolution 
remote sensing could detect, say, which city blocks are hottest and should have 
proportionally higher temperature in ground. Combining these with GIS based physical 
models will produce better maps. 
Advanced model features: Future models might also include features like thermal 
interference between multiple BHEs (for scenarios of high density installations), 
perhaps using superposition models or computational fluid dynamics for clusters of 
boreholes. As deployment grows, understanding how closely boreholes can be spaced 
without major efficiency loss becomes important. Additionally, integrating economic 
modeling (costs, payback under various future climates) can highlight where 
uncertainties have financial significance. For example, a slightly higher extraction rate 
might tip a project from non-viable to viable economically, including those metrics that 
can prioritize research areas. 
Finally, interdisciplinary studies bridging social science could address data gaps in 
market and behavior e.g., surveys or agent-based models on how many people will 
adopt cooling, or how policy incentives might drive different uptake scenarios (which 
feed back into the technical potential realized). Such integration ensures that the
refined models remain aligned with likely real-world trajectories, not just theoretical 
maxima. 
In summary, future enhancements in modeling and data should aim to provide more 
precise, reliable, and scenario-flexible assessments of shallow geothermal potential. 
By tackling the improvements outlined, it supports more confident decision-making. 
These efforts will narrow the gap between the theoretical potential calculated and the 
practical, realized potential, by identifying and reducing the unknowns. As shallow 
geothermal transitions to a mainstream energy source, the models guiding its 
development must likewise evolve from simplistic to sophisticated, ensuring that 
planning is based on solid, site-specific science. Bridging current gaps will ultimately
enable a smoother and more optimized scaling of geothermal heat pumps in 

Results and discussion
66
Germany’s future energy mix.
3.5 Implications and integration with energy system
This chapter’s results stand on the shoulders of prior research, confirming known 
phenomena (like SUHI benefits and regenerative effects) while adding new insight into 
the temporal and spatial dimensions under climate change. The consistency between 
these findings and earlier studies lends credibility. Not seeing any contradictory 
outcomes, which suggests the science of shallow geothermal potential is maturing. 
The contributions mainly refine the understanding for Germany specifically, providing 
quantification that was previously scattered in disparate case studies. 
The implications are clear that shallow geothermal energy is poised to become an 
increasingly important component of Germany’s renewable heating strategy as the 
century progresses. This analysis provides stakeholders with concrete expectations 
(in kW, in %, in maps) to plan around. The big-picture takeaway from this comparative 
discussion is that this work not only aligns with existing literature but also strengthens 
the call for scaling up shallow geothermal deployment. The resource is abundant, 
resilient to climate change, and well-documented by scientific studies now to be a 
reliable heat source for the future.
3.5.1. Implications for geothermal deployment
The scientific findings of this chapter carry important implications for policy makers, 
urban planners, and energy strategists aiming to accelerate the deployment of shallow 
geothermal energy in Germany. To translate the technical potential into reality, 
supportive policies and thoughtful planning are needed to overcome current barriers 
and to leverage the benefits identified (especially the climate change-induced 
enhancements). This section outlines key policy and planning considerations, 
including addressing economic and regulatory barriers, setting targets aligned with the 
results, and ensuring strategic coordination in the rollout of geothermal heat pumps.
Recognizing shallow geothermal as a key renewable heat source: First and foremost, 
policy frameworks need to fully recognize shallow geothermal (GSHPs) as a central 
pillar of the heating transition, on par with solar thermal, heat networks, and energy 
efficiency. Historically, geothermal has been underutilized in Germany providing only 
about 1.2% of heating as of 2018 (Hockenos, 2020). However, scenarios by agencies 
like UBA envisage geothermal (shallow + deep) supplying roughly half of the country’s 
heat by 2050. This is a massive scale-up. This study confirms that technically, this 
scale-up is feasible therefore, policies should be oriented toward achieving these high 

Results and discussion
67
penetrations. For example, the government could set explicit deployment targets for 
GSHPs, such as aiming for 3 million units by 2040, consistent with recommendations 
from think tanks Geo Energy (Agora Energiewende, 2020). These targets create a 
clear signal to industry and consumers. The positive effects of climate change on 
performance could even be used in communication. GSHPs are a future-proof 
technology that will perform better as time goes on a contrast to, say, gas boilers which 
might be constrained by fuel availability or carbon taxes in the future. 
Financial incentives and cost reduction: One of the main barriers identified is the high 
upfront cost of installation, particularly drilling costs (EGEC, 2020). Policies can tackle 
this through robust financial incentives, grants, low-interest loans, or rebates for 
installing GSHPs. Germany has had incentives (e.g., through KfW programs), but 
considering the scale needed, these may need to be expanded and simplified. 
Additionally, incentivizing shared ground loops (community systems) can distribute 
costs among multiple users, making it more affordable. This discussion suggests that 
district or shared systems can be more efficient (Pahud & Belliardi, 2011), but currently 
regulatory and business models for shared systems are underdeveloped. 
Policymakers should create frameworks that allow, for instance, a utility or cooperative 
to invest in a borehole field and sell heat to customers, similar to district heating 
concessions. This might involve adjusting regulations on third-party access to heat 
networks or clarifying ownership of underground thermal rights. Some German states 
have already subsidized communal geothermal projects, scaling that up could 
significantly boost deployment. 
In summary, the policy and planning implications of this work are to create a favorable 
environment for shallow geothermal expansion through clear targets, financial and 
regulatory support, integration into urban planning, and addressing soft barriers like
awareness. If these steps are taken, Germany can unlock the vast technical potential 
The transition from fossil fuels to a resilient, low carbon heating sector has been 
quantified. The convergence of climate benefits (both mitigation and adaptation), 
energy security, and technological maturity presents a compelling case for a major 
national initiative on geothermal heat pumps. With appropriate policy frameworks, the 
positive trends shown in these results (like increasing yields and widespread viability) 
will translate into tangible progress towards Germany’s climate and energy goals.
3.5.2 Integration with energy systems
The results of this study highlight significant opportunities for integrating shallow 
geothermal systems into urban energy networks and building heating/cooling systems. 
In densely populated areas, where both heat demand and anthropogenic heat input to 

Results and discussion
68
the ground are high, shallow geothermal can serve as a core component of 
sustainable urban energy infrastructure. This section discusses strategies and 
considerations for such integration, drawing on these findings and recent literature. 
Key themes include leveraging the SUHIs, combining multiple BHEs in shared loops 
or district heating networks, and balancing heating and cooling to maximize efficiency. 
One striking implication of the results is that urban areas have an inherently enhanced 
shallow geothermal resource due to higher ground temperatures (as noted, up to 5 K 
above rural areas in some German city centers). This SUHI effect can be thought of 
as a form of waste heat storage in the ground, originating from buildings, basements, 
and other infrastructure. Rather than letting this heat dissipate unused, cities can 
actively integrate BHEs to “recycle” it for useful heating (Epting et al., 2018 and 
Stauffer et al., 2013). 
The findings quantitatively support that a 150 m BHE in a city could yield up to 25% 
more heat than the same BHE rurally. Integrating geothermal in urban energy planning 
means acknowledging this stored heat and using it. For example, municipal energy 
managers could map SUHI intensity (perhaps using ground temperature borehole data 
or modeling) and identify zones where BHE installations would be most productive. 
This overlaps with high-demand zones (since city centers also have many buildings 
needing heat), creating a win-win scenario. Install GSHPs where demand and 
resources coincide. Several German cities have already observed substantial 
subsurface warming (e.g. Berlin, Frankfurt, Hamburg, Cologne, Stuttgart, Munich) and 
these could be prime location for pilot integrated geothermal districts (Menberg et al., 
2013). 
Another integration pathway studied by Alavy et al. (2014) is through DHC networks. 
Instead of each building having an individual heat pump and borehole, a network of 
boreholes (geo-field) can be connected to serve multiple buildings via a shared loop 
(Chambers et al., 2020). The results indicate that such networks could dramatically 
increase the share of demand met by geothermal. Walch et al. (2022) found ~85% of 
heating could be covered with DHC vs 55% with individual systems. 
The advantage comes from load diversity and efficiency in a network, the 
simultaneous peak demand is lower relative to total capacity (not all buildings peak at 
once), and waste heat from cooling one building can recharge the ground to help heat 
another. This is effectively seasonal and spatial load balancing. Strong support is 
given for integrating shallow geothermal into low-temperature district heating grids in 
urban developments. Such systems, often called “5th generation district heating and 
cooling” or cold district loops, operate at near ground temperature and use 

Results and discussion
69
decentralized heat pumps at buildings (Lund et al., 2014). The boreholes act as the 
thermal source/sink for the network. The spatial findings can guide where to implement 
these e.g., neighborhoods in Berlin with high SUHI might implement a network of 
dozens of 150 m boreholes beneath parks or parking lots, feeding a communal loop. 
The “geothermal battery” or “geoexchange field” concept in cities is essentially an 
integration of many BHEs to support an entire community’s heating (and cooling) 
needs (Fraser-Harris et al., 2022). This work suggests that in high-potential urban 
zones, a relatively small land footprint (a field of boreholes) could supply a large portion 
of local heat which is a compelling use of underground urban space. Urban integration 
also involves combining shallow geothermal with other technologies in hybrid systems.
For example, studying by Formhals, (2022) combining BHEs with solar thermal 
collectors on roofs can be synergistic. In summer, excess solar heat can be injected 
into the ground via BHEs (raising the ground temperature further) to be recovered in 
winter (Zhang et al., 2021). This is a form of seasonal thermal storage (Pavlov & 
Olesen, 2011). 
The climate-change augmented results imply that the ground will already be warmer 
going forward, so adding solar recharge makes it even more effective. The project in 
Canada (the Drake Landing Solar Community) have shown the viability of BTES fields 
charged by solar (Mesquita et al., 2017). In urban Germany, one could envision 
building-integrated systems where solar panels or waste heat from AC systems feed 
into the boreholes. This as an extension of the results as assumed passive climate 
warming and passive SUHI warming if actively warmed by design, the borehole field 
becomes a powerful storage that can reach higher temperatures (e.g. 15-20 °C by 
winter) and thus yield higher heat pump efficiency and output. 
Integrating control systems to manage this (heat pumps that switch to charging mode 
in summer) will be key. The increased warming of the ground is great for heating, but 
if cooling is needed, a too-warm ground is less effective as a heat sink (Schüppler et
al., 2019). Therefore, integration must account for both, in a city that needs cooling 
(e.g. office buildings in summer), extracting that cooling (injecting heat into ground) 
helps the heating side. 
Infrastructure and policy integration are also crucial. For example, city authorities can 
streamline permitting geothermal boreholes in new developments and encourage 
developers to include communal geothermal loops. In some cities, heat planning is 
now mandatory (as of 2024), and shallow geothermal should be integrated as a 
component. The results help by providing a map of where it works best, so cities can 
zone or prioritize those areas. Integration with urban energy systems also means 

Results and discussion
70
training and coordination. HVAC engineers, urban planners, and utility companies 
need to collaborate on designing systems that cross traditional boundaries (building 
vs utility). A district geothermal system may be owned/operated by a utility or 
cooperative, rather than individual building owners, which is a different model but one 
that could accelerate adoption by reducing individual upfront cost barriers (Gil & 
Moreno, 2019).
It’s worth noting that integration with urban environments has ancillary benefits too. 
Shallow geothermal is virtually invisible and quiet (boreholes are underground), which 
is a big advantage in dense cities where space and noise are concerned. This means 
integration can often happen with minimal disruption e.g., boreholes can be drilled 
under parks, under sidewalks, or even under building foundations (energy piles). 
Urban planners can incorporate geothermal boreholes into infrastructure projects (like 
when a road is dug up, install boreholes under it before repaving). These integrative 
approaches amplify the deployment potential significantly. Some German 
municipalities (e.g., in Munich) are already mapping public lands for geothermal 
potential. 
These findings about optimized operation (shallow vs deep BHE mix) also inform 
integration. Rivera et al. (2017) suggested using shallower BHEs for base load and 
fewer deep ones for peaks. In an integrated system, this could translate to hierarchical 
design. A field of shallow boreholes supplies, say, 70% of annual heat (efficiently and 
renewed by surface heat), while a handful of deeper boreholes or a backup boiler 
handles the extreme peaks. Integration thus might involve a hybrid shallow-deep 
geothermal system within an urban energy scheme. Deep geothermal (aquifer or deep 
boreholes tapping >1000 m) can provide high-temperature heat but is costlier, so using 
shallow for the bulk and deep only for peaks could be cost-optimal.
In conclusion, integrating shallow geothermal into urban energy systems is not only 
feasible but highly beneficial. This chapter’s results provide quantifiable incentives for 
doing things like urban ground heat is abundant and getting more accessible with 
climate change, and when networked, shallow geothermal can cover most of the urban 
heating and cooling needs. To achieve integration, systematic planning and innovative 
business models are required. For example, heat utilities that install and operate 
communal borehole fields, selling heat as a service to building owners. 
Policy support will be key to facilitating these models. Demonstration projects in 
various urban contexts (e.g., historic city centers versus new developments) are also 
recommended to fine-tune technical integration The overarching message is that 
shallow geothermal should be viewed as a critical piece of urban energy infrastructure 

Results and discussion
71
in a decarbonizing Germany akin to how one plans for district heating pipes or electric 
vehicle charging, one should plan for geothermal boreholes and loops in city energy 
master plans. By doing so, cities will not only mitigate CO₂ emissions but also harness 
local waste heat and climate effects, turning challenges into solutions on the path to 
sustainable urban energy systems.

Conclusions and outlook
72
4. Conclusions and outlook
This study achieved the stated objectives by building and applying an advanced 
climate-sensitive geothermal modeling framework. The framework integrates CMIP6-
derived surface temperature projections and successfully computes heat extraction 
rates (W/m) and extractable power (W) under two warming scenarios (SSP 2-4.5 and 
SSP 5-8.5) for sites across Germany.
A Python-based semi-analytical BHE model was implemented that ingests timevarying ground temperature inputs from CMIP6 climate projections processed in 
Google Earth Engine. The model simulates heat extraction for three design lifetimes—
50 years, 100 years, and 100 years sustainable operation—incorporating realistic 
thermal boundaries and borehole parameters. This framework enables the 
assessment of geothermal yield under evolving climate conditions at any location in 
Germany.
The generated interactive Folium maps display calculated heat extraction rates (W/m) 
and usable power (W) per 150 m borehole for every grid cell in Germany across both 
SSP 2-4.5 and SSP 5-8.5 scenarios and specified time horizons. These maps vividly 
identify geothermal hotspots (e.g., major cities and sedimentary basins) and colder 
regions, illustrating shifts in potential over time and between scenarios. Users can 
observe, for example, that urban centers like Munich, Frankfurt, Cologne, and Berlin 
exhibit higher extraction rates (light green/yellow hues) compared to rural uplands
similarly red to dark red for higher power output, with all map values slightly elevated 
under SSP 5-8.5 compared to SSP 2-4.5.
The temporal evolution analysis revealed a systematic upward trajectory in heat 
extraction rates driven by subsurface warming under climate change scenarios. The 
model predicts that mean extraction rates under SSP 5-8.5 will rise from ~27 W/m (50 
years) to ~47 W/m (sustainable 100 years) by 2100, versus ~26-46 W/m under SSP 
2-4.5 which is an absolute difference of only a few W/m. Specifically, after 100 years 
of sustainable operation, median extraction rates increase from approximately 46 W/m 
under SSP 2-4.5 to around 48-49 W/m under SSP 5-8.5, indicating a statistically 
significant enhancement of roughly 5% with 25th and 75th percentiles shifting similarly. 
This trend, consistently observed across multiple climate model ensembles, 
underscores the robustness of the climate-driven influence, though the absolute 
increments remain modest.
Projected total usable thermal power per borehole corroborates the identified 
incremental benefits of ground warming. A standard 150 m borehole currently yielding 
approximately 5.0 kW is expected to deliver around 5.5-6.0 kW by 2100 under the 
high-emission scenario SSP 5-8.5. Conversely, the lower-emission scenario SSP 2-
4.5 results in slightly lower outputs, with differences amounting merely to about 100-

Conclusions and outlook
73
200 W per borehole translating to marginal scenario-driven variations (~1-3%). 
Notably, significantly greater improvements (20-40%) are achievable by adopting 
sustainable, balanced extraction practices over long operational horizons, compared 
to short-term, unbalanced exploitation. Even the 25th percentile (lower yield) sites 
achieve on the order of 4-5 kW per borehole under a sustainable 100 years horizon, 
versus ~4.0 kW today, due to warmer ground. Conversely, high-potential sites (75th
percentile) can exceed 5.8-6.0 kW sustainably.
Spatial distribution analyses demonstrated consistent patterns, with geothermal 
extraction potentials closely governed by geological and hydrogeological 
heterogeneity across Germany. Regions with favorable subsurface characteristics, 
such as high thermal conductivity sedimentary basins and urban areas characterized 
by anthropogenic heat influxes, maintain superior extraction rates (~50 W/m or 
greater). In contrast, mountainous terrains and regions with low soil moisture content 
consistently exhibit lower potentials (25–35 W/m). The consistent spatial hierarchy 
highlights that localized geological and hydrogeological conditions substantially 
overshadow the impacts of climate-driven warming differences between climate
scenarios.
Despite the rigor of the applied methodology, this study acknowledges specific 
uncertainties and limitations. While ensemble simulations indicate conclusions, 
inherent simplifications such as assuming homogenous conductive heat transfer and 
annually balanced operational regimes may overlook transient phenomena like 
groundwater advection, extreme climatic anomalies, or seasonal operational 
imbalances. Potential enhancements include integrating groundwater dynamics 
explicitly, employing higher-resolution subsurface characterization data, and adopting 
transient load modelling to refine local yield estimations further. Nevertheless, the core 
finding that climate warming consistently yields a modest positive effect on geothermal 
extraction which is resilient across tested scenarios and model conditions.
The study's implications underscore shallow geothermal energy's resilience and 
enhanced performance under anticipated climatic changes, affirming its strategic role 
within Germany's future renewable energy portfolio. Rising subsurface temperatures 
mitigate long-term thermal depletion impacts, enabling boreholes to maintain or 
slightly augment their extraction capabilities over extended operational timelines. 
While modest per-borehole gains may appear incremental individually, collectively, 
across thousands of installations, they translate into substantial cumulative renewable 
heat capacity enhancements. Moreover, the strategic emphasis on balanced 
operational practices maximizing gains significantly beyond those provided by climatic 
warming alone reinforces the imperative for optimal geothermal system management 
within national energy planning frameworks.

